Barack Obama Will Be the 4th U.S. President Receiving the Nobel Peace Laureate
U.S. President Barack Obama will arrive in Oslo, Norway to spend 26 hours and receive the Nobel Peace Laureate, as the fourth US president in history.
The $1.4-billion-dollar diploma, medal and cheque will receive them, among other things, for the exceptional effort to strengthen global diplomacy and cooperation among nations.
The head of the White House and his wife Michelle will arrive at the Norwegian metropolis in the morning and will have plenty to do.
First of all, a visit to the Nobel Institute, where for the first time he will meet the five members of the committee who, in October, elected between 172 people and 33 organizations.
Later, the presidential couple are scheduled for a meeting with the King of Norway, Harald V, and queen Sonia.
In the afternoon the tour will culminate in a ceremony in which Obama will receive the prestigious award.
He will be the fourth U.S. president to receive it, even if the third-largest who seizes him.
The White House has already announced that, upon receiving the award, it will talk about the Afghan war.
The president doesn't want to avoid this issue, knowing that he is receiving the award as president of a country that is currently at war in two countries.
His war efforts increased even further with the sending of additional troops to Afghanistan, not forgetting to mention any of his critics.
At the ceremony, Obama will also receive the gold medal, diploma and cheque of 10 million Swedish kronor (approximately 24 million Czech kronor).
It is intended to donate charity paraffixes, but it has not yet decided which particular organization.
During his entire program, the President and his wife will participate in a banquet to which they will attend, in addition to the Norwegian Kings, the Prime Minister and 250 other guests.
Obama from the very beginning had a well-regarded attitude towards the prize.
He mentioned, for example, having the feeling of not deserving it altogether.
He also repeated several times that the prize is not only for him, but for all those who fight for the same values as him.
Immediately after the announcement he said it represents "a challenge to take action."
Defendant of nature accused of blackmail
The Litom-ice police accused of blackmail the president of the Civic Association Spole.nost ochránc. pírody Litom-ice (Spouse of Nature's Society).
Last last year he appealed several times against some construction licensing procedures and to withdraw the appeals he demanded money from investors for their own benefit, said Litomice police spokeswoman Alena Romová.
The President of the Society of Defensors of the Nature Litomization is Lubomír Studnieka.
He's currently in custody and faces a sentence of up to three years in prison.
Brno doesn't like containers too much to separate garbage
In Prague, citizens can separate beverage packaging and in the villages of southern Moravia you can find containers to separate garbage from all sides.
What about Brno?
It takes several minutes there to find the respective containers.
I don't want to say in Brno you don't separate the garbage at all.
It seems to me that, to be the second largest city in the Republic, the municipalities are not sufficiently interested in this problem.
Brno is totally lagging behind in garbage separation, not only because of the fact that we citizens can only use containers to separate paper, glass and plastic, but above all because of the number of containers for such waste.
Have you ever tried, for example, to throw a bottle of PET in the center into the right container?
It is almost impossible, above all it requires a lot of time and patience.
The only place I've found so far is in Moravské námstí Square, in front of Potrefená Huse.
Don't let Mr. Onderka and his comrades get angry with me, but I don't think that's too little.
There's no need to mention else, it's also not easy separating the garbage.
That's why so many of my friends prefer not to separate it.
By vagrancy
It's what's going to happen that the containers are far away.
- I'm not surprised.
I myself offered, after a party, to threw several glass and plastic bottles in the trash.
But on Kaunicova Calle I didn't see any red container nearby.
Fortunately, going to the tram I've found the right place.
The garbage, however, was already brimming with the container.
It's still the center of Brno, the most representative part of the city, isn't it?
What's happening is that ecology and aesthetics don't seem to be too interested in Brno's socialists.
They'd rather buy from the city the hockey extra-league, which embarrasses us throughout the republic, and don't buy any more containers or offer the separation of other subjects so that Brno is cleaner.
I'm happy to be able to compare my hypothesis, after browsing on the Internet a little, with official statistics.
According to the EKO-KOM society, Brno is truly the worst in the entire Jihomoravsk  kraj region.
"Paroubek"'s budget took money out of income and sickness benefits
After the left, despite the government &apos; s opinion, added funds for farmers or civil servants, there might not be enough money in the budget for rents, disease subsidies or for construction savings.
But neither for the interests of state debt or international litigation.
This will hurt everyone who governs in the second half of next year, says Finance Minister Eduard Janota.
When the Social democrats and communists imposed the increase for some groups, they trusted that the government subsequently requested a loan for sensitive expenses, incomes or sickness benefits. "That only means postponing the problems for the future, it's laying mines," Janota says.
What's going on is that, when you borrow money, you have to pay more and higher interest and, one day, you have to return the money.
The state doesn't have much choice to raise money.
If it does not reduce social subsidies or state operating expenses, it has to raise taxes.
To everyone to whom the left added now: the firefighters, teachers, farmers.
Later your children too.
People don't have to worry about income or sickness benefits, they'll receive them. 1.8 billion disappeared from these accounts.
But the price to repay will be the increase in public debt; next year there will be only $163 billion in the treasure, but more.
In spite of Minister Janota's effort, the debt will continue to increase alongside interest expenses.
The state will need the money to cover them and it will no longer come by slightly increasing VAT or reducing maternity benefits, as the government did in its anti-deficit package.
Due to dislodging of the money for the benefit of some groups of voters, next year it will also be $5 billion to complete the construction of motorways and railways.
These are, above all, the sectors in which the bidding has already been held and where the construction process has already begun, whose projects cannot be changed and its price cannot be reduced either.
The most that can be done is to keep them, which is not free either.
This means delaying or ceasing all thirty large buildings; the possibility of obtaining European funds is also in danger, warns Ministry of Transport spokesman Karel Hanzelka.
SSD proposed a salomonic solution for the reduction of funds: it will obtain money from the dividends of EEZ, which is mostly owned by the state, and will add the transport fund.
The problem is that the value of the dividend is never secure and it's a bottomless shoot.
Over the past year, 18 billion went to the budget.
Every year of divide dividends are liquidated ecological damage, serve to complete the account for income or serve as a reserve for the prepared reform of the income.
In addition, this trick, according to EU rules, increases the real budget deficit to 5.7% of GDP level.
The carnival of money transfers doesn't end here.
Nearly $3 billion was obtained from the budget to grant various compensations to the victims of Communism and crimes, or for litigation costs, including in construction savings.
It's not clear whether the state will need such funds.
It's a lottery.
"If we lose any international litigation, maybe we'll have to pay for it," Janota says.
Later, the Minister will have to save money within the spring or, at worst, take advantage of the government's budget reserve.
One hundred million are also missing for the Státní pokladne (State Box) project, priced at $2 billion and which has already been initiated. It must allow online monitoring what the various authorities are spending.
The first stint begins on Jan.
If the finance minister doesn't find the money anywhere else, the project will have to be halted and sanctions will arrive, Janota warns.
Your future smartphone will be able to work with two operating systems
The Americans will have a mobile phone in which the user will be able, by pressing a key, to switch between several operating systems.
Revealed plans sound promising.
By just pressing a key, they pass in a few seconds from Windows Mobile to Android.
Precisely this possibility is the objective of the American company VMware, which focuses mainly on the development of virtualizing software for computers.
On your mobile you will be able to carry two user profiles at the same time.
They can switch between them and divide them between home and work.
"They both operate at the same time," said Srinivas Krishnamurti of VMware in an interview for Computerworld magazine.
It appeared in November of last year and several days ago it was demonstrated for the first time.
It will be launched in 2012.
The virtualization of smartphones is no longer any fiction.
VMware has already successfully demonstrated to journalists how the smartphone works with two operating systems.
It was the Nokia N800, modified with a 128 MB RAM, where the above-mentioned Windows Mobile and Android systems operate at the same time.
The development of the new technology for mobile phones is at its peak.
VMware is co-operating with European and U.S. operators to conduct smartphone virtualization tests and these phones will reach customers during 2012.
The lack of snow in the mountains worries hoteliers
It's not just ski keepers in Krkon, they are concerned about slopes without snow.
The lack of snow means people don't reserve ski stays in hotels and pensions.
That's why you can still get free rooms for all winter dates, including for Christmas Eve and New Year's Eve.
We've got plenty of visitors on our pages.
People look at the offers, check the prices, but they're still sccared to book with commitment.
"And if you'll call us, you immediately ask about our opinion, if this Christmas there'll be snow or mud," said Spindl news portal maintainer Info Martin Jandura.
Those who want to spend a New Year &apos; sleeve on a New Year's Eve should no longer hesitate to book your stay.
Those who prefer to save the money and put the quality of accommodation in the second place can try to wait a few more days.
The hoteliers of .pindler-v Ml?n all offer weekly stays for New Year's Eve.
"I think they will continue to be offered, but they will soon give in and offer shorter stays.
"And then the free rooms will be sold quickly," Jandura said.
Ingotours, a Vrchlabí travel agency, continues to offer free beds for all winter dates.
Now what interests us the most is the Christmas week, which isn't yet complete.
Half of the places offered are still free.
That's why we've already prepared a few last-minute Christmas stays," said Ingtours CEO Petr Schiefert.
The Vrchlabí travel agency can also also offer New Year's Eve in Krkonoe, but there are fewer free places.
They are also sold very well in the first fourteen days of February, the remaining winter dates are sold regularly.
This year the interest in the winter stays is damaged due to the lack of snow in Krkonoe.
Guys are waiting to see if there'll be snow.
No one wants to spend the end of the year on the snow-free mountain.
If the slopes remain snowy during the week, the Christmas dates will run out as well," Schiefert says.
On the contrary, it's a little easier to occupy the rooms at the Horizont hotel in Pec pod Sn?kou; last year it was harder.
It's roughly five per cent better.
For New Year's Eve and New Year's Eve, we've got the last free places.
Earking interest increases during the Polish holidays in January and February it also sells well," said Pec's top hotel director Karel Rada.
Eighty-eight percent of New Year &apos; s stays are already busy at the Hotel Omnia, near the central parking lot in Janské Lázn.
On the contrary, at Janské Lázn's most recent hotel at Christmas half of the beds are still free.
This January it was a big surprise for us, as we are at approximately sixty per cent of occupancy capacity.
February has been a little weaker, but as soon as snow drops, the interest in stays in Krkonoe will surely increase, said Erik Sporysch, the director of the Omnia hotel.
For the time being, however, it seems that the hoteliers of Krkonoe will be given the desire for snow waiting.
Temperatures must fall on Thursday and, if there is precipitation, in the mountains it should swirl.
"Budgets will then rise again," reported the Hyromeeteorological Institute of the Czech Republic.
U.S.: Repeat's Mother of Wisdom
It's comical that Barack Obama, the president with the smartest man's reputation, as a diligent student repeats on the Afghan issue his predecessor's strategy, whom he himself took for the dumbest.
When he finally revealed his Afghan approach, eagerly awaited, it was proven to be a copy of the script script used for Iraq by Bush, created three years ago.
Not only in the same text is the locution "equal than in Iraq," but he also did not hesitate to use the title of Bush's January 2007 declaration for his own statement: "A new way forward."
It was he himself who three years ago criticized Bush's idea of sending new military reinforcements to Iraq, the country he is suffering from the civil war. And he criticized it as "an irresponsirificance with catastrophic consequences."
The next few months, however, have shown that Bush's strategy, based on the recommendations of field captains, was the only one possible and successful, so there's no talk today about Iraq in the news.
Bush's reputation didn't get any better, yet he objectively left his successor a much more favorable starting position than expected.
In Afghanistan, Barack Obama expects the miracle to be repeated.
The field captains again called for an increase in the military contingent, especially the commander-in-chief of the Afghan Operation General Stanley McCristal. He, even in spite of the traditional subordinate relations, subdued undecided shooters in the White House into harsh criticism.
He asked for 40,000 men to be able to improve the situation.
The president hesitated for three months, but couldn't think of anything better, so he at least fooled the general over 10,000 military.
He managed to get Europe to pledge another 5,000, although he himself previously had at least $10,000.
The most troubling part of Obama's entire strategy seems like his goal, to start removing troops from Afghanistan in 18 months and finishing this procedure in three years. Obama's entire doctrine generated more questions of the answers it offered.
Many hesitate that the increase will bear the same fruit as the procedure in Iraq.
Despite fratricidal wars in recent years, Iraq's society is relatively well-organised, its leaders respect the agreements.
The situation in Afghanistan is different: it is not possible to rely on anyone and no agreement is fulfilled.
Under these conditions it is difficult to prepare the Afghan security forces, if they are accustomed to changing the boss every day according to their needs.
Political institutions, although formally established, will continue to be only a town of Potemkin, and patriarchal tribal relations will remain.
Balashaing the interests of different ethnic groups is extraordinarily complicated because of confusing relations, even more complicated by Pakistani and Iranian influences.
Don't even establish a state that's "capable of action," a minimum objective of the entire operation, seems very real.
The skyscraper of Obama's strategy is buoyed by his voluntarism.
For the past eight years, the situation in Afghanistan has continued to deteriorate and is now about to erupt.
Only a romantic soul, who doesn't know anything about the reality, can believe that the soldiers sent to the war to "return home soon," will do some miracle here.
From a political point of view, fixing the date of the withdrawal of the troops is pure irresponsibility, since the Taliban will regard it as an indirect declaration of U.S. defeat.
The last NATO summit showed that Europeans are less and less willing to participate in military operations in Afghanistan.
European politicians don't know, and they also don't want to explain to their constituents what the security of Germany or Italy has with the war with the Hindu Kush.
The other factor, capable of radically affecting the situation, is the development of developments in Iran.
If the situation continues to deteriorate and augmented sanctions have no effect on the Iranians, the U.S. will have to make a tough decision: trying to solve the problem with military force or not.
It will be necessary to find the answers precisely when, according to Obama, America begins the triumphant withdrawal of troops from Afghanistan.
However, each such solution will represent a dramatic setback for the Afghan situation.
The only positive point for Barack Obama is the lack of three years before the presidential elections and that is why there's still plenty of plenty of time to find another strategy, if it turns out a total failure.
Goldman Sachs bench executives won't ever receive cash bonuses again
U.S. bank chief executives Goldman Sachs won't receive cash bonuses this year.
The entity announced it to react to the harsh criticism of its salary practices.
The group of 30 senior managers will instead receive shares that cannot be sold within a minimum of five years.
In addition, the actions may be withdrawn in the event that they have assumed excessive risks.
According to Reuters news agency, Goldman Sachs thus became the leader in the effort to unite bonuses on Wall Street with long-term power.
"I suppose Wall Street is fully aware of the longest path it needs to undertake," former JPMorgan-based bank investor Douglas Elliott said.
Trouble lies in the details, he added.
The Goldman Sachs bank faced criticism after detaching almost $17 billion (almost $300 billion from CZK) for bonuses, in the first three quarters of this year.
The total sum of bonuses in the entity for this year, according to Reuters agency, is likely to surpass, despite the measure announced today, the $20 billion.
The high bonuses in the banking sector became, with the arrival of the financial crisis, a sensitive issue in politics.
Britain announced on Wednesday that it is planning to tax only one-on-ever-ever million-stroke of bank bonuses exceeding 25,000 pounds (approximately CZK 712,000).
France is also preparing similar measures.
This year the women received the Nobel laureates in all categories except Physics
Five women, which is a record number, received today in Stockholm from King Charles XVI Gustav of Sweden this year's Nobel laureates this year, in specialized categories and in literature.
In addition to the four scientists, a German writer of Romanian origin, Herta Moller, was also among the winner.
The Nobel Prize in medicine was received by two American anomalies, Elizabeth Blackburn and Carol Greider, as well as his compatriot Jack Szostak for research in the field of chromosomes.
The Nobel in chemistry was received by Israeli Ada Jonath and American Venkatraman Ramakrishna and Thomas Steitz for an explanation of the structure and function of ribosomes.
The last prize, the Nobel Prize in Economics, was received by the American Elinor Ostrom and his compatriot Oliver Williamson for the analysis of an economic report.
The only specialized category, without an award-winning woman, was physics.
Scientists Charles Kao received this award today for research in the field of optical cables, and George Smith with Willard Boyle for the invention of the CCD chip, which forms the basis of all digital cameras, faxes or telescopes.
Each winner picked the diploma, the Nobel medal and the money certificate received.
This amount in each category is up to 10 million Swedish kroner (approximately 25 million Czech kronor).
In the case of having several winners in each category, the prize is distributed to each other.
U.S. U.S. President Barack Barack Obama was greeted this afternoon today afternoon in Oslo, Norway.
In his speech he acknowledged the controversy of having been rewarded, as he finds himself at the beginning of his career and is also at the head of a country that participates in two wars: in Iraq and in Afghanistan.
In order to defend his policy he added, however, that in certain situations wars are essential to achieve peace, even if its price is high.
Minister Janota considers the resignation. Klaus invited him to the Castle
President Václav Klaus will receive Finance Minister Eduard Janota on Friday in the morning, who is considering his stay in the government, as he does not agree with the version of the state's budget for next year, approved on Wednesday by the Chamber of Deputies.
Presumably the budget, and perhaps Janota's stay in the cabinet as well, will be the themes of his meeting on Friday.
The budget is also criticised by Klaus.
According to him, the deficit is too large, which continues to deepen the public finance crisis.
President Radim Ochvat &apos; s spokeswoman reported.
Regarding his tenure, Janota wants to negotiate Monday with Prime Minister Jan Fischer.
According to the budget law, prepared to be signed by Klaus, the Czech Republic should govern with a deficit of 163 billion kronor.
The House of Representatives, however, on Wednesday approved the left-wing proposals for transfers of funds and increased the money for civil servants' salaries, social services and direct payments to farmers.
The government doesn't agree with the Social Democrats' proposals, as they covertly increase the budget deficit.
Between the left on the one hand and the government, Klaus and the right on the other, there was a serious conflict of internal policy. Due to this conflict, the future activities of Fischer's bureaucratic cabinet are beginning to be controversial, the form of which was agreed in the spring by the SDG, SSD and the Green Party.
Critics say that the budget, in the version approved by the Chamber of Deputies, de facto liquidates the package of savings measures, imposed in the autumn by the Fischer government to curb public indebtedness.
Klaus cannot but does not have to sign the law on the state's budget; today he declared during a breakfast with businessmen that politicians have long neglected "the serious problem," i.e. "unsustainable deficit" of public finances.
According to him today they don't have a good solution anymore.
Only a strong government that can be supported by a broader consensus between the political parties could improve this situation, the president says.
SDG leader Mirek Topolánek said the budget is appalling and that the government should think about its future existence.
The Vice-President of SDGs, Petr Neas, said for TK that the concept of the bureaucratic cabinet with the support of SSD, ODS and the Greens evidently no longer works.
Prime Minister Fischer reacted and described similar words as "strong sentences."
The head of "SSD," Ji'í Paroubek, reacted to Topolánek, saying that the budget deficit is appalling, prepared for this year by the former Topolánek government. It will surpass 200 billion kronor.
On Wednesday "SSD" said the approval of the state budget for next year was successful.
The people were happy, too.
Mandela in Clint Eastwood's New Movie, Played by Morgan Freeman
South Africans say Hollywood's new film, Invictus: Invincible, retaliates a lot about the country, its struggle and its victories, although some critics say the leading roles are played by American actors.
History speaks of sport, race relations and Nelson Mandela.
Relentless anti-aparthetic anti-apartheid racist regime in the South African Republic and its first black president, Nelson Mandela, is played in his biography by U.S.-winning actor Morgan Freeman.
Freeman said 91-year-year-old Mandela asked him to play him in Eastwood's film.
Matt Damon plays another important role.
I said, "When I'll interpret him, I'm gonna have to have access to you.
The seventy-two-year-old actor, who received the award for his role in Eastwood's drama about the boxing world Milion Dollar Baby, played the role of the leader of the movement for the abolition of slavery, who ended up being a slave, the fictitious U.S. president, and even of God at Evan Almighty. But he rarely represented someone who is still alive and, for many people, is as important as Mandela.
The former South African stateswoman spent 27 years in prison for his efforts to end apartheid in the South African Republic.
He was liberated in the year 1990 and for four years became president of the country.
He received the Nobel Peace Laureate in 1993.
Invintus - Invincible is a Latin word and the title of a poem by the English author William Ernest Henley, published in 1875.
The challenge was to talk like him.
"The film is a real story about Nelson Mandela, who joined his forces with South African rugby team captain Francois Pienaar to help unite the country. Mandela, the new president, knows that after apartheid his nation is still racial and economically divided.
He thinks he's capable of uniting his people through the sport, and that's why he's determined to unite rugby players, who belong in the world to outsiders.
The South African team managed to reach the World Cup in 1995.
Freeman worked for several years for Mandela's story to reach theaters.
"He didn't have another goal to play this role in such a way that it was as close to reality as possible," Freeman said.
Naturally, the biggest challenge was to talk like him.
The actor said that if he and the world's politician were from the same area, he would have tried to reunite with Mandela, dine with him, be behind the scenes before his speech.
The most important thing was that he wanted to shake Mandela's hand.
"I discovered that by squeezing your hand, I receive your energy, it is transmitted and I had the feeling of knowing what you're feeling," he said.
It is important for me to try to become a different person.
Matt Damon plays the role of Francois Pienaar, the captain of the national rugby team, in which white players predominated.
The actor said he had had six months to prepare for a role in the tough rugby rugby world.
A great surprise for him was, when he finally first reunited with Pienaar at home.
I remember knocking on the door, he opened and the first thing I said to Francois Pienaar in my life was: "In the film I look much louder."
In spite of the obvious differences between the characters of the actors and their real models, and under the management of Director Eastwood, Invictus: Invincible received positive reviews and there is also talk of ambitions for the Incar.
Daily Variety film critic Todd McCarthy summed up his assessment with these words: "It was a very good story, a very well-made film."
In film reviews on the website rottentomatoes.com, Invictus received 76 per cent of positive assessments.
Splendour's secret over Norway solved: Russians tested on a missile
A special brilliant effect of unfamiliar origin was observed on Norway.
Russia's Ministry of Defence ended up acquaintment yesterday acknowledging yesterday that near the borders with Norway it tested an intercontinental ballistic missile.
The plunder failed again. The "Bulava"-type missile, previously intended to be the pride of the Russian army, is becoming the nightmare of Russian generals and Prime Minister Vladimír Putin.
Not even his repeated presence in the missile tests has had the desired effect on the success of test flights.
Bulavá, in most cases, did not float or an air failure occurred.
In the Russian newspapers they write "Bulavá" as "a missile that doesn't fly."
A non-flying missile but shines shines.
The 13th test did not occur at the beginning of any problems.
At the end of the flight there were technic technical problems in the aircraft again.
Probably the engine exploded in the third stage of the missile.
This time "Bulavá" was thrown from nuclear submarine Dmitry Donskoy, which floated immersed in the White Sea.
This type of missile makes it possible to launch from a boat that's moving and submerged at the same time.
That's why it's likely the mysterious light over Norway, caused by an unidentifiable flying object, would actually be the flawed "Bulavá."
The Norwegians did not hesitate from the very beginning that it was a Russian missile.
Generals this time defend each other by stating that you can't just talk about a failure.
The first two phases of the missile endured and the breakdown occurred in the third.
In the previous cases the engines broke down immediately in the first stage.
Although none of the pitches took place entirely without errors, only six of the thirteen were failed, according to the Ministry of Defence's management.
The success of almost fifty per cent is full of optimism to the generals and that is why they continue to state that "Bulavá" will one day fly without problems and that it will even manage to carry up to ten nuclear hypersonic blocs, whose maximum total weight is 1.15 tons.
The legendary Fetisov has signed at the age of 51 an annual contract with CSKA of Moscow
Legendary defenseman Viacheslav Fetisov will reappear at the age of 51 to a professional hockey meeting.
"The former world champion, Olympic champion and Stanley Cup winner will help his CSKA Moscow team and arguably will play Friday in the KHL duel against St. Petersburg.
Fetisov completed his professional career at the age of forty in 1998 and currently presides CSKA.
After Denis Kulyash was injured, we urgently need another defender.
Fetisov is constantly practicing and is committed to helping the team.
"We just need to sort out some formal issues," Sergei Nemchinov, the Moscow club's coach, was quoted by the AP agency.
It's not yet clear whether the renowned player's return will refer to just one game.
"I think Fetisov is one of those who manage to raise the mood of the other players," Nemchinov said.
According to AP if Fetisov returns to the ice streaks, he will become the longest-serving professional player in Russian hockey.
Legendary striker Gordie Howe played in the NHL for Hartford at the age of fifties, during the entire season, and scored 15 goals and 26 assists.
The eldest in the five-famous Fetisov Players, Kasatonov - Makarov, Larionov, Krutov is one of the best and most successful hockey players in history.
Now Playing for the Soviet Union he won two Olympian golds, seven world championships, triumphed in the Canadian Cup and the junior world championship.
In the second part of his career he also had successes in the NHL: playing with Detroit won the Stanley Cup twice.
Since 2001 he has been a member of the Hockey Glory Hall.
Hyundai met the requirements of the trade unions.
The management of the automotive factory in Noovice agreed with the trade unions to cancel all the additional work.
Precisely because of the frequent additional work, the unions on Monday declared a strike emergency.
The information has been confirmed by Petr vank, the spokesman for the Hyundai automobile factory.
The management, however, wants for this year to agree with the trade unions one more Saturday shift.
"We can't re-ensign to our manufacturing plan, since it's essential. That's why we proposed additional voluntary work for Saturday, December 19, that is, we will work on two shifts," Vank said.
According to the Sedmi-ka.cz server, the company offered 400 CZK bonuses to employees who work Saturday's shift.
The bonus will also be paid last Saturday, November 28.
According to the company, the company decided to change the way it motivates its employees with the additional work offered.
Employees can charge the bonus for overtime or they can take a day off on December 28, i.e. extend the Christmas holidays in one day, Vank said.
In addition, all Saturday's shift employees will receive money for the trip and food.
Because the additional work was the most urgent and problematic point of the comments and complaints of the trade unionists and strikers, who stopped the manufacture last week, the management yesterday afternoon immediately decided to cancel all the additional work for the month of December, Vank mentioned.
Employees of the automobile factory were forced to work two more hours in addition to eight-hour shifts and this situation lasted almost every day until last week.
After signing the agreement, the trade unions will cancel the strike emergency.
Another negotiation between the management of the factory and the trade unionists must take place on Monday morning.
The head of the trade unions Petr Kucha said on Wednesday that if they committed to meeting the requirements and signing the document, they are willing to cancel the strike emergency.
The situation in the car factory worsened last week on Wednesday, when approximately 400 employees spontaneously went on strike because of frequent additional jobs.
They also protested against poor payment conditions and alleged bullying.
Coroner said employees had to work overtime to meet the requirements and be able to supply the automobiles.
The trade unionists in their declaration of Monday's strike emergency also demanded that none of the employees, who last Wednesday interrupted manufacturing, be penalized in any way.
With regard to supplementary work, trade unions require the company to minimise it.
In addition, this year they require a salary of 5,000 kronor.
The automobile maker Hyundai factory currently has 2,000 employees.
Serial manufacturing began last Nov.
Until last September it manufactured approximately 80,000 automobiles; its current productive potential is 200,000 cars a year.
A Czech discovery: an effective substance even in the 'deleted' HIV virus
The team of Czech and German scientists tests new compounds capable of moderating the spread of HIV in the organism
The main advantage of this improved substance is the fact that it is also effective against drug resistant viruses.
In some cases this is more important than the fact that the compound isn't as effective in a common variant of the virus, like some already accessible medicines.
AIDS disease cannot be cured so far.
However, the lives of patients can be significantly prolonged thanks to a mixture of medicines that prevent the replication of the HIV virus in the body.
However, its use has many side-effects.
If the replication of the virus is not completely suppressed, the so-called resistant viruses will be created; the drugs no longer work.
The work of the experts of three Institutes of the Academy of Sciences of the Czech Republic, V.CHT Prague and the University of Heidelberg demonstrates the possibility of dominating the resistance of the virus.
They showed that the substances called metallic cassation have an effect on albumin, which is responsible for the spread of the HIV virus.
The metal-ocarbers are compounds of boron, hydrogen, carbon and cobalt.
These compounds block the propagation of the virus in a different way from all the drugs used today, and that is why they can solve the problem of endurance.
In their work the scientists find new, "improved" compounds, prepared, knowing the molecular mechanism of their link to the albumin of the virus.
The metal-cardiarcharies have a single three-dimensional structure: two polyhedral cages made up of boron, hydrogen and carbon atoms are joined by the metal atom, in this case by cobalt.
A traitor and resistant virus
HIV proteasis is an albomine of the HIV virus and is essential for the life cycle of the virus.
Without the proteasis of HIV does not occur the fractionation of the HIV/HIV virus and uninfectious corpotrusers of the virus would not be created.
"If we stop HIV proteasis, we also stop the propagation of the virus in the patient's body, the scientists explained in a press release published by the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry at the Academy of Sciences of the Czech Republic.
In an article published in the scientific journal Journal of Medicinal Chemistry, the scientists describe a series of compounds, in which two pairs of cages (see table) are joined by a short organic series, which is still systematically modified.
A little weaker but more effective
Proceeds of the effect of this series of HIV proteasis substances were tested, as well as against their resistant variants, obtained from patients with HIV.
The effect of metalliccarbon on the common variant of the enzyme isn't as strong as in clinically used medicines, however they don't lose their effect on resistant variants, in which the drugs often don't work.
The mechanism of the unique effect, its other qualities such as biological and chemical stability, the low toxicity and the possibility of other chemical modifications, makes metalocarbon compounds interesting for research with the aim of proposing effective anti-HIV drugs, says Pavlína "ezá'ová, the head of the structural biology laboratory at the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry and the Institute of Molecular Genetics in Academy of Science.
Scrambling over the budget. SDG attacked Fischer, he defends himself
