The pneumococcal vaccine will be free and voluntary
The pneumoccal vaccine from January will put an end to the usual commute.
As a first vaccine it must be free for families and at the same time voluntary.
However, there are already opinions that the mandatory vaccine would become more effective.
Pre-sparencies for the introduction of the free pneumococcal vaccine are culminating.
The Institute for Medicinal Products is already setting the maximum prices for vaccines, which will be covered by insurance companies.
The experts are also clear about the vaccination procedure.
The New Vaccine
Free vaccinitudes for children born in August
As of January, the pneumoccal vacci will be available free of charge by children born from 2 August last year.
The condition is that you haven't received a dose of the vaccine before.
Those who started with the vaccine last year or earlier have to cover other doses of the vaccine as well.
The first dose of the vaccine has to be received between the third and the fifth month of a child &apos; s old.
Last last year, a quarter of the families voluntarily vaccinated their children against pneumococcals.
Parentomers, however, paid 1600 kronor-and-eight doses.
To vaccinate the youngest children it takes four doses.
Usually the experts estimate that the insurance companies will spend on vaccines of 300 to 40,000 kroner annually.
Without clutch they will save on treatment, which is needed for unvaccinated children.
Until now the state has spent approximately 500 thousand annually for all the vaccination of the children.
The covered vaccination was initiated by the so-called Janota package.
Pneumococcus is the cause of otitis media, but also of serious infections including meningitis and septicaemia.
Of pneumococsal infections in our country every year according to research in hospitals 28 children not over the age of 10.
In addition to the ow to the Pneumoccal Vaccine Caution in Kids was introduced in approximately 40 countries.
Will the vaccine be mandatory?
At the same time, however there are opinions, that this vaccine could be mandatory in the future.
Starting in January it must be covered by the insurance and voluntary companies.
The mandatory vaccine would be better, said Rudolf Kalovsk, the head of the Nahlas Civic Association.
Their association has been spreading pneumococcal vaccines for a long time.
The experts at an international conference, in which our association participated, were not entirely satisfied with the fact that we have the voluntary vaccine.
It's not entirely ideal.
On the other hand we're happy to achieve at least this.
"I think this is the path we now have to take," Kalovsk said.
The Ministry of Health, however, does not wish to change the vaccination procedure against pneumococcals.
"At the moment we don't think about it at all," his spokesman Vlastimil Sr?e said.
The voluntary vaccine has its discombates, doctors say
pediatric society President Odborná spole.nost prakt.ch d'tsk-ch léka, Hana Cabrnochová recognizes that the voluntary vaccine has its disadvantages.
If all the children would receive the vaccine, it will be possible to reason about the reduction in the number of the doses. "In the situation, when the vaccine costs such a high amount, we relatively understand the political decision to have started vaccination as voluntary," says Cabrnochová.
The experts are expecting more than 80 per cent of young parents starting next year to let their babies vaccinate against pneumococcals.
The pneumoccal vaccine is a big change also because as the first vaccination in the children is covered by health companies, not by the state.
Insurance companies should spend more money on prophylaxis, to then save on treatment, Cabrnochová explains.
Physicians will have to buy vaccines on their own.
Pediatricians, however, are concerned about the purchase of the vaccines.
Previously, the vaccines had to be bought by the insurance companies and distributed to the doctors, just as when the state gives them other vaccines.
Some MPs, however, were scared that insurance companies could "get the money out of health insurance."
Physicians will therefore have to buy the vaccines and wait, until they are paid for by the insurance companies.
It is estimated that pediatricians will invest 40,000 a month in vaccines, which is a remarkable sum.
"It won't be easy at all," Cabrnochová says.
The son of an U.S. artist implicated "in the theft of a museum"
Renowned US fantasy artist Frank Frazetta has been accused of stealing paintings valued at $US20 million ($12 million) from his father's museum.
Pennsylvania police allege Alfonso Frank Frazetta was trapped while carrying 90 paintings in his vehicle and his trailer.
They allege that Frazetta and another man used an excavator to erupt the museum in the Pocono Mountains region.
