The Labour party defends tax increases in the budget
Ministers have defended tax hikes and spending cuts in the pre-budget report, facing criticism from the opposition, businesses and trade unions.
The Conservatives said Alistair Darling "squandered" an opportunity to show his seriousness about cutting the deficit, delaying decisions until after the election.
The finance minister has also faced stiff criticism for hurting low- and middle-income workers.
Among its main proposals are an increase of 0.5 per cent in social security expenditure and a ceiling of 1 per cent on public sector wage agreements from 2011.
Angry for Social Security
Trade unions have lamented that low-wage-wrenched workers are chased by a recession generated by others, and warned of future "problems."
The increase in Social Security spending, which will grow by around three billion pounds a year, has angered the business community, which claims that this is a tax on jobs, when the focus should be on the economic recovery.
The increase, limited to those earning more than EUR 20,000 a year, will affect approximately 10 million workers.
KEY PUNTOS OF THE ANTERIOR REPORT TO THE BUDGET
Social security with an increase of an additional 0.5 per cent from April 2011
Economy will contract more than expected 4.75 per cent this year
New 50 per cent tax on banker's bonds
The 1-point corporate tax hike for small-ticket firms was discarded
Ex-tax relief for electric cars and wind turbines
The state pension would increase by 2.5 per cent
According to the estimates of the Ministry of Finance, a person earning 30,000 will lose $90 a year and whoever earns 40,000 will lose 190, while a person earning 10,000 a year will earn 110.
Ministers said their goal of ha halving the deficit by 2013 involved "difficult decisions," but they insisted that 60 per cent of the additional tax burden would fall to 5 per per cent of higher wages.
Finance Minister Stephen Timms has denied tax hikes and delineated spending cuts were a "grain of sand in the desert" compared to what was needed.
"These are great numbers, it will allow us to halve the deficit, which is absolutely essential over the next four years," he told the BBC.
Mr. Timms said he was confident that the economy would grow again by the end of the year.
However, the Labour party's forecasts for future economic growth, which will have an impact on borrowing and how quickly the deficit will shrink, have been in doubt after the finance minister was forced to change the early figures.
In his statement, he stated that the economy would likely contract by 4.75 per cent this year, much worse than the 3.5 per cent decrease predicted in April, while borrowing would be $3 billion higher than previously estimated.
"Wasted opportunity"
The Conservatives, who will launch a new advertising campaign on Thursday, warning about the Labour party's "debt crisis," said the loan application projection of $789 billion over the next six years was untenable.
Ministers had failed to unsubstantiate a credible plan on the way they would return the money, they added "cyclingly" dodging difficult options until after the elections due in June 2010.
"They don't include anything significant about what they're going to do," Finance Minister Philip Hammond said.
All important announcements will be made after the elections, win whoever wins."
Roger Bootle, Deloitte
The Labour party insists that schools, hospitals and the police will be protected from future cuts, unlike the Conservative proposals, which would seek a further short-term deficit reduction.
But the Liberal Democrats claimed that the money raised by tax increases and spending cuts would be used to sustain next year &apos; Spending, not to reduce the deficit, arguing that the projects were like "screams."
BBC political publisher Nick Robinson has said the chosen timing for adverts, such as the rise in inflation in some disability subsidies next April, mentioned above, would leave the finance minister exposed to accusations of election maneuvers, as the money could simply be retrieved the following year.
However, the Labour party insists people are being provided more in need and the situation will be revisited in September 2010.
"Fake Spons"
Although ministers didn't publish spending data after 2011, economists said it was clear the adjustment of public spending would only be felt in the medium term.
The Respected Institute for Fiscal Studies said the finance minister's figures involved substantial cuts in many areas, potentially including future transport, higher education, science and defence.
"It's really postponing the sorrow to later on," said its director, Robert Chote.
"Everall major announcements will come after the election, winning whoever wins," added Roger Bootle, an economic consultant for Deloitte's accountants.
"That's been the fake pre-budget report - the markets realise this."
