Amnesty International Condemns "abuses" Iranian "abuses"
Human rights in Iran for the past 20 years have always been equally deficient, according to a report by campaign group Amnesty International.
The report details "models of abuse" of the regime, before and after the presidential elections held in June.
A man cited in the report said he had been beaten and burned with cigarettes and another said he was threatened with rape.
Iran has rejected previous criticism of its human rights record.
Officials have said that such criticism has a political foundation.
The thousands of people arrested and the dozen assassinated in Iran after President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad &apos; s election provoked the largest street protests since the 1979 Islamic Revolution.
A dozen people have been convicted, and prosecutors say at least five people have been sentenced to death.
BBC Tehran correspondent Jon Leyne, who is now based in London, says that during the protests, Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei acknowledged some allegations of abuse, ordering the closure of the Kahrizak detention centre.
But since then, the authorities have shown very little tolerance for criticism, according to our correspondent.
"Phut confession".
Amnesty International cited the account of Ebrahim Mehtari, a 26-year-old computer scientist, who said he was accused of "working with Facebook networks" and protesting against the outcome of the elections.
"They often pluck me in the face" according to his words.
"They burned me with cigarettes under my eyes, in my throne, my head... they threatened to execute me and humiliated me."
Amnesty said that after five days he signed a fake confession and was released and abandoned in the street, still bleeding and semi-conscious.
In August, defeated presidential nominee Mehdi Karroubi said that some protesters detained after the election had been tortured to death in prison, and others had been raped.
Iranian officials denied the rape allegations, but admitted there had been abuses.
Amnesty also cited the case of an ex-defendant, who said he was held in a container along with 75 other people for more than eight weeks at a detention center in Kahrizak.
Amnesty acknowledged that the Iranian parliament and the judiciary had set up committees to investigate uneasiness of post-election and the government's response, but said the mandate and powers of the agencies were confusing and inquiries by the Parliamentary Committee had not been made public.
The group said that at least 90 people had been detained in the past three weeks to prevent further demonstrations.
