Can Islam be French?
Pluralism and pragmatism in a secular state.
At a time when Swiss voters have called for a ban on the construction of minarets and when there is a widespread warning about the supposed Islamization of Europe, John Bowen, an American academic, has written a detailed and studied report on the possible integration of Muslims (who are actually integrating) into one of the continent's most outspoke-opathic societies.
Some readers will come to this new book as admirers of the author's latest work "why don't French like veils" (2006), an elegant and detail-blooted study of a problem that divided and worried the country for a decade-and-a-half, whose effects are still perceived today.
Mr. Bowen's latest book has a broader and more ambitious perspective.
As a good anthropologist, he wants to know not only what politicians and the media are saying about Islam in France, but what's really happening.
He has spent months in mosques, schools and colleges that now provide the 5-6 Million Muslims in France with what Mr. Bowen calls "Islamic Spaces."
He is a good listener who reproduces debates between teachers and students about the questions that interest them most.
Should a Muslim get married in a mosque or in a town hall (or both)?
Should they teach young Muslims about the evolution and rights of homosexuals?
Can a Muslim Woman Marry a Non-Muslim Man?
Is it legitimate for a Muslim to use an interest-based banking system to obtain a mortgage?
It's these seemingly mundane issues, he argues, things of daily life that worries the media rather than political dramas.
The author identifies a new generation of imams, professors and intellectuals, none of them renowned, with the possible exception of Tariq Ramadan, a Swiss-born academic and Arab Muslim scholar.
This new generation tries to open the debate on how at the same time being a good Muslim and a good citizen in a modern secular society.
