Mr. Anderson looks inside the extraordinary, tiny world of the affluent system within the Arctic ice, made up of saline drips that compresses as it freezes.
But from the bear on the surface to the microscopic marvels inside, they are all doomed once summer icing is retiring, what's believed to happen at some point between 2013 and 2050.
In just a few months... these could be us.
Marines among the 6,000 placed wreaths in Arlington graves
19-year-year-old De'Angello Robinson travelled seven hours by bus to lay a single wreath at the tomb of a soldier he'd never met.
For him and the other Marines who traveled from Camp Johnson in Jacksonville, N.C., to Arlington National Cemetery to Decorate Tomb, it was a day to pay homage to men and women who were once standing in their boots.
"Within a few months, from where they're boarding us, these could be us," Robinson said about the fallen soldiers.
If it's us, I'd want somebody to do the same for me, so I'm just trying to show respect.
He was one of more than 6,000 volunteers who gathered Saturday morning to lay wreaths at veterans' graves in several sections of Arlington Cemetery.
In 1992, Morrill Worcester, the owner of the Worcester Wreath Company in Harrington, Maine, kicked off the event when he and others decided to decorate several hundred graves at Arlington Cemetery.
In what's already an 18-year streak, Morrill and his wife Karen make the trip every year stopping in different cities along the way, to officiate events dedicated to soldiers and victims of terrorism.
The Worceters also founded a non-for-to-profit, Wreaths Across America, which has extended the event to other states.
Earlier this year the volunteers placed more than 16,000 kronor in the tombs of Arlington Cemetery, areas of the Pentagon, graves at Fayetteville National Cemetery in Arkansas, Battery Park in New York City and the United Airlines Flight 93 commemorative site in Shanksville, Pennsylvania.
For the second year, the Walmart Foundation donated more than $150,000 to buy and carry the wreath.
Charleen Hunt, 70, of Westminster, said she made the trip to Arlington in honour of her husband, a career-delayed soldier.
As long as her husband isn't buried in Arlington, Hunt said this was his way of giving retribution to those who made sacrifices.
"That's a modest way that civilians can support our troops," he said.
It's like visiting a tomb of family.
Wrapped in layers of warmwengwear, the volunteers walked through the cemetery's spacious meadows and hills.
The volunteers headed to the oldest graves of World War I, World War II, and the Vietnam War, because they don't receive as many visits as new graves, organizers said.
The atmosphere was cheerful as the children played and the pair took their hand, as they carried the cooled pine to the tombstone.
Each circular wreath was of deep green, with a small red loop straddled at the top.
Nearby, Section 60, where most veterans of the Iraq and Afghanistan wars are buried, was virtually silent.
"Association for the Soldier") donated 1,000 kroner to decorate the section, but the bleak atmosphere of the current wars invaded the area.
"People cried openly, others prayed, and families and friends leaned towards each other in glacial air.
Some loud sculpted nams sculpted in the stones, while others leaned in to see small black and white plaques placed inside wet mud, for those whose tombstones have not yet been completed.
Sandra Lockwood was one of many moms wielding tears in her eyes in front of her son's grave.
He spent eight hours from Zanesfield, Ohio, to visit the tomb of Sgto navy gunman David Shane Spicer, who died in combat in July.
"No one should ever forget why we're free . ." my son paid for it," he said.
"Very after my death I want somebody to remember."
U.S. officials arrested a U.S. government contractor
The U.S. government has detained a U.S. government contractor distributing mobile phones and laptops in the country, State Department officials said on Saturday.
The contractor, which has not yet been identified, is working for Bethesda-based Development Alternatives Inc.
The company works on projects for clients such as the U.S. Agency for International Development and the World Bank.
Consular officers along with the U.S. Interests Section in Havana are seeking access to the detainee, who was arrested on December 5.
The specific charges have not been made public, although according to Cuban law, a Cuban citizen or a foreign visitor can be arrested for almost anything deemed "dangerous."
All so-called counter-revolving activities, which include minor protests and critical writings, carry the risk of arrest.
Anti-government graffiti and speeches are regarded as serious crimes.
Cuba has an incipitant blogger community, led by popular commentator Yoani Sanchez, who often writes about how she and her husband are followed and harassed by government agents.
Sanchez has repeatedly requested permission to leave the country to accept prizes, but his permission has been denied.
The detention of a U.S. contractor could likely cause tensions between the communist government of the Castro Brothers in Cuba and the Obama administration, which has been seeking a "slow-paced rapprochement" to improve relations with the island.
The New York Times was the first to release news of the arrest on Friday night.
The new U.S. policy emphasizes that if the Cuban government takes concrete measures, such as the release of political prisoners and the creation of more space for the opposition, there will be reciprocity from the United States.
Cell phones and laptops are legal in Cuba, even if they are new and coveted products in a country where the average salary of a government worker is $15 a month.
The Cuban government granted ordinary citizens the right to purchase mobile phones only this year; they are mainly used for text messages, as a 15-minute telephone conversation would cost the daily wage.
The use of the Internet is extremely limited on the island.
It's available in dear hotels and foreign visitors to the hotel, and at some government establishment, such as universities.
Cubans who want to connect often have to give their names to the government.
Access to many websites is restricted.
The detention of an U.S. man in Cuba is unusual.
Gloria Berbena, a press officer at the U.S. Interests Section in Havana, said the bulk of a handful of U.S. citizens in jail in Cuba are behind bars for crimes such as drug trafficking.
Berbena said she was unable to provide further information about the arrest.
The arrest and detention are clearly misguided.
An activity that in any other open society would be legal, such as distributing free mobile phones, in Cuba is a crime, said José Miguel Vivanco, director of the program of the Americas of Human Rights Watch, who recently published a tough report on freedoms in Cuba, called "Nuevo Castro, himself Cuba," a reference to Raúl Castro's location as leader of the country, to replace his sick older brother Fidel.
Vivanco said that the defendants are often arrested, prosecuted and jailed in one day.
He stated that any solution would probably be political, and that the Cuban government often provokes a backlash in the United States, just when the two countries begin to have more dialogue.
Pakistani officials unravel a plot to send men to Afghanistan
On Saturday, Pakistani authorities neutralized the alleged mastermind of a plot to send five men from Northern Virginia to Afghanistan to kill U.S. troops, saying they hope the case can help unraminate an extensive network of terrorist recruiters, traveling the Internet searching for radicalized youth.
Investigators said they were persecuting a mysterious insurgent figure, known as Saifullah, who invited the men to Pakistan after discovering that one of them made comments approving terrorist attacks on the internet video site YouTube.
Saifullah directed the men once they were in Pakistan, attempting to help them reach the remote area in Pakistan's tribal belt, which is a familiar place for al Qaeda and its terrorist training camps.
But a Pakistani intelligence official who had been briefed on the case said on Saturday that Saifullah was unable to persuade al Qaeda commanders that the men were not part of a CIA plot to infiltrate the terrorist network.
They were therefore isolated for several days in the eastern city of Sargodha, far from the torturous north-north-northed mountains, which have become a terrorist paradise.
They were seen as part of an undercover operation.
For this reason they were rejected, said the official, who spoke from an anonymity due to the delicacy of the case.
The official said that the men did not immute themselves and were even attempting to obtain proper approval to access al Qaeda camps, when Pakistani law enforcement authorities arrested them.
The case of the five men, who remain in Pakistan and are being interrogated by the FBI, underlines the critical role of recruiters in the identification of potential terrorists and, perhaps more importantly, determine those who are trustworthy.
Since the September 11, 2001, U.S. intelligence has prioritized trying to infiltrate people within al Qaeda.
Recruiters of the organization act as keepers, not by letting pass those who don't demonstrate a serious commitment to holy war, and those who might be spies.
Analysts said al Qaeda analyzes with special attention to aspiring American recruits.
But for the organization they represent an enormous attraction, because of its potential to gain access to U.S. goals and because of its propaganda value.
But Evan Kohlmann, a senior analyst for the U.S.-based NEFA Foundation, said terrorist groups have also become much more wary in recent years about those they admit, because U.S. intelligence agencies have become adept at their recruitment methods.
"If you are trying to infiltrate someone in these groups, what better way than to follow the recruitment model how many have already followed?" Kohlmann said.
It's morphed into a much more web-based model.
