Inside the convention center, people gathered around the television screens to watch the march throughout the afternoon.
But the outcry didn't appear to penetrate the consciousness of key officials such as Su Wei, China's chief negotiator on the climate.
Asked if he thought the demonstration was having a constructive impact on international deliberations, he replied in English, "I really wasn't aware of the situation."
Then he kept saying in Mandarin, "Because the meeting place is large, I can't hear what's going on outside."
He pointed out that it depended on everyone's perspective to define whether the march was being harmful or beneficial.
He said this shows the attention of the general public and from different sectors to the issue of climate change.
On the other hand, "it can also be said that it affects the negotiations, or the freedom of other people.
Proof data reveals lingering racial slurs
Last week's Fed R&D notebook on mathematical performance was very good news for the Capital District's public schools.
Although the District is still a long way from the best-performing systems in the country, the rating notebook showed that fourth- and eighth-graders slipped at a faster pace over the last two years, compared to the cities of Atlanta, Chicago and New York.
But what's engraved in the recent National Assessment of Educational Progress figures is the lingering achievement difference between African-American students and white students, both locally and nationally.
