Every-Day mathematical mathematicians from kindergarten to sixth grade, which emphasizes real-life games and experiences, which was introduced in the District under former superintendent Clifford Janey, who is credited with some progress reported by ENPE last week.
"I just don't think we need to keep working to offer the best interventions to those students who are below the grade level," Rhee said.
Others say ENPE results highlight the question of whether Rhee can continue to increase the overall performance of the system and still provide additional money and resources for underperforming schools, which will be needed to reduce the difference.
"There's kind of sneaky dilemma here for Michelle Rhee," said Bruce Fuller, a professor of education at the University of California at Berkeley, who studies urban test scores.
The solution to the conventional policy is to allocate its resources and administration reforms to schools located in the poorest sections of D.C.
So the objective can generate political repercussions that go against that important timetable.
But the problem, of course, is that, like other urban superintendents, she tries to hold on to the black-and-white-and-white middle class.
Our focus is on ensuring that we build a system of excellent schools.
We need to strengthen our neighborhood schools, so that all families, regardless of wherever they live, are confident that their children will be able to access excellent public education.
Janey, now superintendent of Newark schools, said in an interview this week that an essential element to ending the performance difference is lengthening the traditional public school year, now 180 days in the District, to compete with autonomous schools.
Almost 200 days must be reached to have the strength of the change, said Janey, who reached an agreement with the teachers' union last year in an 185-day year, which he sees as an "indicator" to seek a bigger increase in the next agreement.
To make higher education a part of more future
The tour bus arrived at the University of Gettysburg with a lofty scratch.
Graciela Rodriguez, 12, descended and blinked for a moment before the white columns, brick facades and emerald meadows.
Graciela &apos; steaters had barely graduate from a high school in El Salvador.
Until recently, Graciela herself, who was born in Silver Spring and lives in Riverdale, had never set foot on a college campus.
Even so, when she and the group's other eighth-graders scouted Gettysburg, where enrollment costs $38,690 a year, their attitude was less that of impressed guests than that of enthusiastic potential students.
"Yeah, this is where I'm going to be," exclaimed Graciela, who would like to study medicine, when the guide announced they had entered the science building.
"Hey, seriously?," he said thoughtfully, "when the guide talked about the school's low teacher-student proportion.
The University of Pennsylvania was the seventh that the children had visited on their three-day trip, and they had already completely absorbed their deliberate message: The question isn't if you'll go to college.
It's the question of which one.
While there's a growing concern about the large number of U.S.-born children of Hispanic immigrants dropping out of high school or becoming pregnant in adolescence, there's also hundreds of thousands accessing the college education they need to access the middle class.
In fact, one in five of these "second-generation" Hispanics graduate from university, a remarkable achievement since many of their immigrant parents, mostly Mexicans and Central Americans, entered the United States without finishing high school.
Its success stories are important, the researchers say, because they indicate the way forward for a generation that will play a major role in the country's labor force.
Analyzing higher-performings say they often have a natural affinity for the school and an innate tendency to succeed.
Many also have high-pronged targets for their children and find ways to make up for their lack of familiarity with U.S. schools.
But tutoring programs can also play an exceptional role in helping Graciela and millions of children like her get to college, particularly if efforts are sustained over time.
Observing low-income children whose parents don't have the experience and skills to help them navigate the system, it follows that any intervention at any time won't solve it, said Patricia Gándara, a researcher at the University of California, Davis who has examined Latino students.
We need to think about providing a support network for these children, from kindergarten to high school.
The federal program that funded Graciela's university tour is a useful example.
Messed like GEAR UP, it provides more than $300 million a year for local school systems to run university prep programs, which begin when low-income students are in middle school and continue until high school ends.
Since 1999, the program has aided more than 10 million students, with more than 60 per cent attending the university, according to the U.S. Department of Education.
The Iraqi Oil Ministry reaches agreements with 10 foreign oil companies
Despite worries about the violence and political instability, the Iraqi government managed to attract major oil companies to rebuild its fragile infrastructure, during two auctions that ended on Saturday.
The 10 agreements the Iraqi Oil Ministry reached with foreign oil companies suggest that China, Russia and European oil companies are prepared to play a leading role in the restoration of Iraq's oil industry, mutilated by decades of war and sanctions.
U.S. companies walked away having pushed into just two of the 10 auctioned sites.
Seven U.S. companies had paid to participate in the second auction, which began on Friday.
The only one who filed an offer lost.
Two U.S. companies reached agreements for deposits auctioned in June.
The poor portrayal of U.S. oil giants at the opening of Iraq's oil industry surprised analysts.
"Iraq has finally opened its doors after six years of war, and instead of U.S. companies, there are Asians and Europeans at the forefront, said Ruba Husari, editor of the Iraq Petroleum Forum, an online news center.
It'll be plenty of time before anything else is on offer in Iraq.
Security worries accentuated by massive co-ordinated bombardments on Tuesday, and political instability as the U.S. military retreated, likely prevented U.S. oil companies from risking more forcefully in Iraq, which has the world's third-largest crude stockpile, analysts said.
U.S. companies were in some cases at a disadvantage, because rivals, particularly Chinese and other government-controlled energy companies, have considerably lower labor costs and are more likely to take risks because they don't respond to shareholders.
Exxon-Mobil and Occidental Petroleum Inc were the only U.S. companies that reached agreements with the ministry.
Leading U.S. firms such as Chevron and ConocoPhillips, which have tightened ties with the Iraqi Oil Ministry and provided technical advice in recent years, moved away empty-hand.
The Russian companies Lukoil and Gazprom were the main protagonists in two of the contracts awarded this weekend.
The National Petroleum Corporation, owned by the Chinese state, offered in more contracts than any other company and achieved large investments in contracts for two main deposits.
"We know that China is on its way to becoming a major economic and technological power, Oil Ministry spokesman Assam Jihad said.
We are confident that the Chinese company will be alongside its competitors and will fulfil its commitments to Iraq.
Pre-selected companies to submit proposals made bids that then clashed with the barrel subsidy that the ministry was prepared to pay to increase production above the current levels at each site.
