"We need to accelerate the readiness of the Afghan security forces and send the correct signal to the Afghans so they can trust the international community," Eide said in a telephone interview from Kabul.
Conrision must be long-term.
The Norwegian diplomat also lobbied the U.S. and other U.S. powers to increase the number of international civilian officials, working together with Afghanistan's political transition.
The increase in the military sector needs to be reflected in the civilian sector, he said.
Eide said he wasn't resigning, he was simply fulfilling a promise he made to his family in March 2008, to spend only two years in Kabul.
He said he wanted to notify the guster.
U.N. General Ban Ki-moon, so he would have time to find a replacement.
