Gates: Iran will face additional sanctions
Secretary of Defense Robert M. Gates said on Friday that the world powers will soon impose "significant additional sanctions" against Iran because of its failure to hold talks on its nuclear ambitions.
Gates, addressing a group of approximately 300 U.S. troops in northern Iraq during a one-week trip to war zones in Afghanistan and Iraq, downplayed the prospect of military action against the Islamic Republic.
"There's no good options in Iran," he said, in response to a soldier's question about the probability of such an event.
One of the things that weighs me is that, if we've learned anything from Iraq over the last six years, it's the inherent unpredictability of the war.
The Obama administration is considering a package of sanctions aimed at Iran's military and political elite, but Gates noted that some sanctions could also affect the wider Iranian population.
He argued it would be necessary to "an incentive and disincentive package," "to persuade the Iranian government that they would actually be less secure with nuclear weapons," because "their people will suffer enormously" the sanctions.
On Friday, in a statement by White House spokeswoman Robert Gibbs, the administration rejoined European leaders that Iran will face "believable" consequences if its nuclear program doesn't fully comply with U.N. Security Council and its nuclear-drinking body, the International Atomic Energy Agency.
Iran insists that it wants to develop nuclear knowledge only for peaceful purposes.
At talks in Geneva on October 1, he said he would re-negotiate the restriction of his nuclear program and agree to leave a substantial part of his stockpile of enriched uranium, in exchange for desperately needed fuel for a medical research reactor.
The administration has lobbed for such an agreement as a way to build trust between the two sides and gain time for the negotiations.
But since then, Iran appears to have moved away from templar agreements, in part, experts say, because Iran's command is divided on the possibility of committing to the United States.
"Frankly, I believe that Iran's hardening with the international community on some proposals they actually accepted at the beginning of October has united the international community, including the Russians and the Chinese, in a way that they haven't in terms of significant additional sanctions against the Iranians, Gates said.
U.S. President Obama has set a December 31 deadline for Iran to respond to the proposals, before rethinking other options, even continuing what Foreign Minister Hillary Rodham Clinton once called "devastating sanctions."
In a statement issued in New York, Iran's mission to the United Nations denounced what it called "unsubstantiated and unsubstantiated accusations" by some members of the Security Council on Iran &apos; s nuclear activities, and said it is willing to continue talks with the United States and five other world powers, "in order to achieve an appropriate, long-term solution."
Gates, who was due to return to Washington last Friday, met with Iraqi Prime Minister Nouri Al-Maliki in the morning, before blowing himself into the oil-rich Kurdish region of Iraq to meet with troops in Kirkuk and Kurdish officials in Irbil.
Tensions remain high between the Kurds and the Arab majority in Iraq, in particular with regard to borders, property rights and co-participation.
Gates urged both sides to diminish the potential for the conflict, to avoid any delays in U.S. projects, to reduce the number of U.S. troops from 115,000 to 50,000 by the end of August.
Gates also sought to alleviate Kurdish anxiety about the pending reduction. U.S. officials cited Gates' words to Massoud Barzani, the president of the Kurdistan Regional Government: "We will preserve his security, prosperity and autonomy within a unified Iraq.
We won't abandon them.'
In an aging China, a change of course
Wang Weijia and her husband grew up surrounded by provoking propaganda posters who lectured them on the fact that "Mother Earth is too tired to support more children" and "one more baby means one more tomb."
They learned the lesson so well that, when Shanghai government officials alarmed by the low birth rate and aging population of their city suddenly changed course this summer and began to incentivize young couples to have more than one child, their reaction was instantaneous and steadfast: "There's no way.
We've already given all our time and energency just for a kid.
"We don't have anything left for a second," said Wang, 31, a human resources manager with an 8-month-old son.
More than 30 years after China's one-child one-child policy was introduced, creating two generations of notoriously crooked children, sprawling one-shocked, affectionately nicknamed "little emperors," a demographic crisis is emerging in the country.
The average birth rate has plummeted to 1.8 children per couple, compared to the six when the policy became effective, according to the Division.
U.N. demographics, meanwhile the number of residents aged in their 60-and-and-a-grey is expected to explode from 16.7 per cent of the population by 2020 to 31.1 per cent by 2050, which is well above the global average of about 20 per cent.
Disagreement is worse in wealthy coastal cities with very cultured populations, such as Shanghai.
Last year, people ages 60 and older accounted for nearly 22 per cent of Shanghai's registered residents, while the birth rate was less than a kid per couple.
Xie Lingli, director of the Shanghai Family Planning and Municipal Population Commission, has said fertile couples should have babies to "help reduce the proportion of the aging population and mitigate a future labor shortage."
Shanghai is about to be "so old (not so rich, however) as developed countries such as Japan and Sweden," he said.
Sacred relief
Wrapped up in the country's constitution in 1978, China &apos; s only one-child policy is arguably the most controversial mandate introduced so far by the Communist Party.
Customers who violate the policy face enormous fines, up to three times their annual salary in some areas, and employment discrimination.
Chinese officials have endorsed the policy for its contribution to avoiding critical pressure on its natural resources, while human rights advocates have denounced abuses in the imposition of the policy.
In rural areas, some officials have forced pregnant women with a second child to undergo abortions.
In addition, many couples have had abortions based on the sex of the fetus, leading to an unnificent male-female proportion.
In recent years, population officials have gradually softened their stance on one-child policy.
In 2004, they allowed for more exceptions to the rule, including urban residents, members of ethnic minorities and cases in which both the husband and the wife are unique children, and in 2007 downplayed many of their hard-liners.
Qiao Xiaochun, a lecturer at the Institute for Demographic Research at the University of Beijing, said that central government officials have recently been discussing even more radical changes, such as allowing couples to have two children if one of the spouses is an only child.
In July, Shangai became the first Chinese city to launch an aggressive campaign to incentivize more births.
Almost overnight, the posters ordering the families to have a single child, were replaced with copies of regulations detailing who would be suitable to have a second child and how to apply for a permit.
The city's government sent family planning officials and volunteers to visit couples in their homes and slide leaflets under the gates.
It has also promised to provide emotional and financial guidance to those who have chosen to have more than one child.
Family planning officials say the response has been very unwise.
Disappointing response
Although officials in a rural town on the outskirts of Shanghai argue that they observed a moderate increase in requests from couples who want a second child following the launch of the campaign, the more urban districts do not indicate any changes.
The municipality of Huinan, with a population of 115,000, for example, is still receiving only four to five applications per month.
Disappointed Shanghai officials say that, despite the campaign, the number of births in the city in 2010 is expected to reach only about $165,000, slightly higher than in 2009, but lower than in 2008.
Feng Juying, head of the family planning committee at Caolu Municipality in Shanghai, said financial considerations are probably the main reason why many people don't want any more children.
"They wanna give the best to their first child," she said.
Yang Jiawei, 27, and his wife, Liu Juanjuan, 26, said they would love to have two children and are legally empowered to do so.
But like many Chinese, they only have the insufficient health and life insurance provided by the government.
"Without a social safety net," they argue, such an option would be irresponsible.
"People in the West incorrectly see the one-child policy as a rights issue," said Yang, a construction engineer whose wife is seven months pregnant with the couple's first child.
Yeah, they're stealing the possibility of having more than one kid.
But the problem isn't just a political issue.
That's the money.
Other couples cite psychological reasons for their doubts.
Wang, the human resources steward, said she wants an only child because she too was: "We were the center of our families and we were accustomed to everyone taking care of us.
We don't get used to caring for others and we don't really want to take care of others.'
Chen Zijian, 42-year-old, who owns a translation agency, explained it more bluntly.
"For parents with two middle-class, middle-class careers that reduce the birth rate, he said, it's about having enough success to be selfish.
"People between the ages of 20 and in their 30s grew up watching their parents' struggle during the early days of China's experiment with capitalism and they don't want that kind of life for themselves," he said.
"Even a single kid requires plenty of parental time," she said.
A mom has to give up at least two-year-old from her social life.
Then there's space issues, "they have to remodel their apartment," and the strategist "has to have a curriculum prepared for the best nurseries when the kid is nine months old."
"Most of his friends are willing to face this once," Chen said, "but not two."
"Ours is the first generation with the highest standard of living," he said.
We don't wanna make too many sacrifices.
U.N. panel outlines plan to reduce emissions
The U.N.-sponsored climate conference, characterized so far by defiant postures and mutual recriminations, drew renewed attention on Friday with the dissemination of a document that foresees ambitious reductions in greenhouse gases over the next 40 years, with the industrialized nations carrying on their shoulders most of the burden in the short term.
The text, which could provide the basis for a final political agreement to regulate greenhouse gases, highlighted the remaining hurdles and highlighted the way forward.
But it was seen as an important breakthrough in a negotiation that is running out of time, with more than 100 world leaders arriving in Copenhagen next week.
Elaborated by an ad hoc U.N. task force, the text doesn't talk about how much money the rich countries would give to the poor to cope with short- and long-term global warming.
And this offers a variety of options for key questions, which include how developed economies and major emerging economies would trim their carbon production, and what would be the limit of the global temperature rise that political strategists would be willing to tolerate.
"That gives a lot of flexibility to the process," said John Coequyt, Washington's top representative for the Sierra Club.
Michael Zammit Cutajar, who drafted the six-page document, summed up a 180-page negotiating text to concentrate on what the top U.N. climate official, Yvo de Bóer, described as "the big picture."
It displays the profiles for a possible agreement, in which industrialized nations would collectively cut their emissions by 2020 by 25 to 45 per cent, compared to 1990 levels, while the major developing countries would reduce theirs during the same period by 15 to 30 per cent.
Together, countries would slash emissions by between 50 and 95 percent by 2050.
The European Union also swept talks on Friday, pledging to provide $3.6 billion a year over the next three years, to help poorer countries adapt to the impact of climate change, from floods and drought to deforestation.
Either way, the same kind of verbal fireworks that have dominated the conversations last week was unveiled on Friday.
U.S. Special Climate Envoy Todd Stern rejected claims calling for mandatory cuts in greenhouse gas emissions for industrialized countries, compared to volunteers from major emerging economies, if not funded by the developed world.
The maneuver indicated that the Obama administration is taking a tougher line with China on what Bush administration officials did just two years ago.
The U.S. won't make an agreement without major developing countries moving forward and taking action, said Stern, who also lamented that the text didn't do enough to ensure the cuts could be verified by external observers.
Stern made his comments an hour after China's Deputy Foreign Minister He Yafei said America's top climate negotiator lacked "common sense," or was "very irresponsible" for saying earlier in the week that the U.S. wouldn't help China economically cope with global overheating.
With the future economic trajectory of the world's leading powers at stake, fault lines have erupted both within the developing world and between the industrial and emerging economies.
The current battle is both to save individual economies and to save the planet, with China and the U.S. bragging about their respective obligations, while the poorest nations insist the world's twenty-four most influential countries are unassuming the scientific imperative to take bravest action.
Ricardo Ulate, a delegate of Costa Rica, said it is not surprising that the main powers are discussing who should pay the expenses to limit greenhouse gases, even though vulnerable countries have become more aggressive seeking to contain the big emitters responsible for their actions.
"This is clearly a game where new economic hegemony is unfolding," said Ulate, who also works as a regional climate change adviser in Mexico and Central America for International Conservation.
Some of the countries most vulnerable to the impact of climate-changing indicated that they would continue to push for a legally binding treaty in Copenhagen, even if most major participants say the talks will ultimately produce a political agreement.
The 43-member Alliance of Small Island States drafted a 24-page predatory treaty proposal early on Friday morning.
Artur Runge-Metzger, who leads international climate negotiations on behalf of the European Commission, said the push by small island nations has "put political pressure on the entire political process," in part because they are now unified and demand action from emerging economies, such as China and India.
Conversations assumed a new urgency as delegates focused on the fact that they must resolve most exceptional issues before the heads of government come to close a deal.
High-profile officials, such as Indian Environment Minister Jairam Ramesh and China's deputy prime minister, descended the planes and rushed to the corridors of the Bella Center, to closed-door meetings and press conferences, to be able to delineate the claims that will be arbitrated over the next week.
The fierce mess of the meeting (where every day 13,000 people come in and out of the convention center, the activists who play the guitar present night shows mocking the countries they say are being sold, with preliminary proposals from hand to hand rather than mail), poses a challenge.
Intensity alone builds: almost all key ministers are here at the moment, and on Wednesday 60 heads of government will be in Copenhagen.
"We are entering the major leagues," said Carlos Manuel Rodriguez, vice-president for international conservation policy.
The heavyweights are coming.
Britain's first 225-km/million train redefines that sentiment on Monday
At 5.13pm on Monday morning, a whistle will pierce the obscurity to mark a train exit that marks a revolution for travel in Great Britain.
The nation's first high-speed intercity train will reach 225kmph, when it passes like lightning from Ashford, Kent, to London's St Panrcas station, covering 93 kilometers in 38 minutes.
Lord Adonis, the Transport Secretary, will then be joined by Lady Kelly Holmes aboard Javelin's first inter-urban service outside London.
He hopes that the new service will finally connect Great Britain to an international network of fast-paced, reliable trains and connect the great conurbations from the Midlands, the North and Scotland to London.
Necidentated remote areas would become thriving suburbs, business travel times would be shortened and the shift of planes and cars to the railway would reduce domestic coal emissions, as well as ease congestion on the roads.
In theory, the three main political parties believe in this vision.
But the tens of billions of pounds is needed to build a new national rail network, the inevitable battle of planning and fears over environmental damage, can still curb Britain's high-speed lines.
High Speed Two (HS2), a company set up in January to provide the Government with a feasibility study, will deliver its report to Lord Adonis on December 30, according to the Times.
This will provide a detailed itinerary of the next stage planned for the high-speed network.
The new line linking London to the West Midlands will be drawn within a five-metre radius in urban areas and areas where the environmental impact can be controversial.
In rural areas, the plan will be drawn within a 25-metre radius of a final route.
HS2 is also expected to establish three options for a wider high-speed network heading north.
Lord Adonis will respond in the spring.
Prepared option that is being proposed is a Y-shaped set-up, in which a single high-speed line would reach the Midlands of the West.
The line would be divided into or near Birmingham, with a branch heading west from the Pennines to Manchester and Scotland, and another north-east to Sheffield, Leeds and Newcastle upon Tyne.
A single line would go into Scotland.
It seems that this will be the most beneficial option," said a member of the railway.
Upon completion, the travel times between London and Edinburgh would be reduced to two hours and 40 minutes.
The first instalment, which wouldn't open before 2025, would allow commuters to travel between London and Birmingham in 49 minutes, compared with just over an hour today.
The proposals would allow the trains to travel at 402 km/h, making Britain's network the fastest in Europe.
The initial route is being designed to withstand 400-mett-long trains, capable of carrying 1,100 people.
At least 18 trains could operate every hour on a London-Birmingham line.
This means it would require a London terminal capable of receiving 20,000 passengers per hour.
In view of the capital's space restrictions, it is expected that an existing station will be expanded to adapt to the high-speed network.
Rail industry pundits say only St Pancras International or Euston stations offer that potential.
Lord Adonis will announce whether he believes the scheme should proceed, but extensive public consultation and planning procedures mean a final decision will not be made until after a general election.
Even if it starts the work, the network would have to be built in sections.
Trains would initially be forced to sweep the fast lines through existing lanes north of Birmingham, keeping journey times between London and Scotland for more than three hours.
This is seen as the crucial point necessary to ensure a change from planes to trains, a reference point for the railway revolution.
