Separatist Abkhaz elected president although Georgia denounces "fraud"
This Saturday Abkhazia, the pro-Russian separatist region of Georgia recognized by Moscow more than a year ago, elected president; however, Tbilisi denounces "fraud."
Some 131,000 people were summoned to participate in the first election after the Russian-Georgian war in August 2008 for control of South Ossetia, another Russian-recognized Georgian separatist region.
Polls closed at 5 p.m. GMT.
According to officials of the Abkhaz Electoral Commission, the turnout was 58 per cent hours before the shutdown.
The first provisional results would be announced overnight from Saturday to Sunday.
The five successful candidates, including the outgoing president, Sergea Bagapch, categorically reject the idea of reunification with Georgia, which disapproved the voting scrutiny.
These elections are a farce.
For his part, the Georgian president, Mikheel Saakachvili, "feels that Abkhazia has fallen under total Russian occupation," said its spokesman, Manana Manjgaladzé.
In addition to Russia, only Nicaragua and Venezuela recognized the independence of 216,000 inhabitants, while the rest of the world considers it part of Georgia illegally occupied by Russian troops.
Upon its disengagement from Georgia during the 1992-1993 conflict that resulted in several thousand deaths, Abkhazia tries to demonstrate through this election that it is ready to join the international community.
"This process is a stint of our new life, our new era as an independent state," Svetlana Kvartchia, a 54-year-old historian who claimed to have voted for Bagapch, told the AFP.
She was among the electors who went to the polling station at a school in the capital, Soukhoumi, a nice white building with palm trees at the entrance.
The topic of Russian domination is the central theme of the presidential election in this region with sumptuous landscapes on the shores of the Black Sea.
Huge planks show Bagapch next to Russian President and Prime Minister Dmitri Medvedev and Vladimir Putin in different parts of this tiny 216,000-strong territory.
In Gali, in the west of the country, district chief Beslan Archba warmly embraced Russian leaders who came to control the development of the scrutiny.
In a brief ceremony, he said: "We, the people of Abkhaz, are grateful for Moscow's support, and very particularly the determination to recognize Abkhazia's independence.
"I hope that his country will quickly be recognized by the United Nations," said Alexe Ostrovski, a member of the Russian parliament, raising his glass of vodka.
For its part, the opposition represented in the election by former Vice-President Raoul Khadjimba and two businessmen, Zaous Ardzinba and Beslan Boutba, criticizes the agreements signed with Moscow last year, such as one that cedes control of the Abkhaz railways to Russia for 10 years.
The fifth candidate, Vitali Bganba, maintains that he does not support the current power or the opposition.
It takes a 50 per cent majority plus a vote to winning the election.
If no candidate reaches this threshold, a run-off will be organized two weeks later.
