Delay in payments: Disgust among prison guards, it is expected to take action
The prison guard syndicates on Friday called for a "society mobilization" to demand payment before the end-of-the-year festivities, premiums and overtime, which was postponed until January for budgetary reasons.
The Prison Worker Force (2nd guard syndicate) asked off-of-duty personnel to join the demonstration on Tuesday morning in front of the prisons and "block them" to denounce the theft of their assets.
The prison CGT (3rd trade union of the profession) convenes them to "get together in general assemblies in the different prisons to achieve a mass mobilization," as it calls "disgraceful and unaccceptable" the non-payment in December of the "extra hours, night bonuses, Sundays and holidays" worked in October.
And the first guard syndicate, the Federal Autonomous Penitentiary Union (Ufap/Unsa), "convenes the general mobilisation (...) in order to increase discontent and angry."
The Penitentiary Administration Directorate and the Ministry of Justice explain that overtime increased greatly in 2009 and not all were accounted for, but they claim that the balance will be paid in January.
At the end of the day, the Chancellery reported that Michale Alliot-Marie had sent a letter to the on-call syndicates that the payment would take place next month.
"I don't hesitate the efforts made by the staff for the operation and modernization of our prison system," the Minister of Justice wrote in this letter.
I requested the Directorate of the Penitentiary Administration to immediately implement a set of measures aimed at ensuring that this situation generated by a poor assessment of the number of overtime does not occur again in the future.
The December payment will then be scant, except for management staff who enjoy the benefit of the celebrity bonuses for functions and objectives, and who "always blame the budget requirements," the Ufap/Unsa deplored.
"It's a real fuss," the FO FO junction of Marseille said, announcing its decision to boycott "all meetings and convocations."
The staff will not come to work on the corresponding Sundays and public holidays if those in October aren't paid in December, the same will happen with the extra hours, they also threaten FO Rh?ne-Alpes-Auvergne.
At a time when the tasks and missions are increasing without taking any more troops, it is more than regrettable to dare to attack the salaries of the staff, the Bordeaux Prison CGT estimates.
According to the trade unions, not in all regions the same thing happens, as some are luckier than others.
But in any case, the result isn't the expected one, Ufap Secretary-General Jean-Franeois Forget said.
For Christmas, Obama says he makes "better gifts" than those he receives
U.S. President Barack Obama, who will celebrate his first Christmas as a family in the White House, confided to the famous television presenter, Oprah Winfrey, that she came to a simple conclusion about the gifts: "I make better gifts than I receive."
During a special interview that the ABC network will broadcast on Sunday night, the President and his wife Michelle explain how they plan to celebrate the end-of-year festivities in their prestigious new residence.
Even Bo, the little four-legged companion of the family, will be from the game.
"Daddy Santa also loves Bo," said Michelle Obama, clarifying that the dog will receive "of course" a gift, according to excerpts of the program transmitted in advance.
And Michelle Obama disagreed with her husband's claims about the quality of the gifts she receives: "There's no way, last year I gave you nice gifts," she said, provoking the president.
True, he was forced to admit it before answering with humor pointing to the First Lady's pearl necklace: "Who gave you that?"
During the one-hour interview, in a relaxed atmosphere with the famous cheerleader who devidently supported Barack Obama's presidential campaign, the president and his wife also recalled their favorite childhood gifts.
Barack Obama remembered a two-speed bike, as well as a basketball that his Kenyan father, whom he rarely saw, gave him for Christmas.
"I remember that time when my father came to meet us at Christmas and gave me a basketball," the president said.
Only many years later, I found out that he'd gave me that ball.
The First lady remembered her doll house.
"I had no clue how to organize a house, so I put all the furniture online against the wall, rather than accommodating them around the home, yet I adored that doll house," Michelle Obama said.
Johnny Hallyday re-operated, RTL says he's in an induced coma
Johnny Hallyday intervened again in Los Angeles due to significant "injuries" resulting from an operation in France, but the situation is "under control" according to his relatives, while RTL claims that the singer was subjected to an induced coma to avoid pains and complications.
The 66-year-year-old rocker was intervened again from Wednesday night to Thursday at the Cedars-Sina hospital in Los Angeles where some doctors found "injuries resulting from the discal hernia operation" carried out in Paris on November 26, said his press service in the same city, indicating that these injuries "need for a restorative operation."
However, the singer's press service tried to convey reassurance by stating that the situation was "under control," and reported that a new medical party would communicate within the next 48 hours.
According to RTL, the sponsor of the singer's last tour, Johnny Hallyday agreed, however, to be subjected to an induced coma to avoid "pains and complications."
In less than 48 hours when the patient awakens, we will have more certainty about his condition, RTL adds.
The press service, interrogated by the AFP, did not confirm or comment on the information.
RTL also states that "according to some sources" it wouldn't have had any cannule introduced after the first operation in Paris.
The practitioner had undergone surgery at the Monceau Park International Clinic due to a discal hernia.
The intervention, scheduled for a few days, was practiced by neurosurgeon Stéphane Delajoux, a well-known doctor in the artistic environment.
On Thursday night, Delajoux declined to comment on the information transmitted by RTL.
Singer Johnny Hollyday was admitted on Monday to Cedars-Sina Hospital in Los Angeles for an infection resulting from Delajoux &apos; s intervention.
Singer Eddy Mitchell, a close friend of Johnny Hallyday, explained Thursday on RTL that his friend was going through "a bad time," but said there was "no problem" because the rocker is "a fighter."
"She called me just before traveling to Los Angeles, and I told him it was silly to get a 12-hour plane after such an operation, it's not possible," Eddy Mitchell said.
On Wednesday night, the producer of the musician, Jean-Claude Camus, tried to reassure himself by claiming that Johnny Hallyday "satisfactorily reacted to antibiotics" and that the infection was "controlled."
Camus said he had chatted to Laeticia, the musician &apos; s wife, and that he had not noticed any particular concerns.
But the singer's health problems multiplied since July when he was hospitalized for nine days following a fall on his yacht.
Two months later, to general surprise, Johnny Hallyday revealed he had been operated on a "colon cancer" after a minor infection.
At the end of September, the singer recaptured, as planned, his long "Gira 66," presented as his farewell to tour.
But since then, every health problem has been discussed exaggeratedly. Fans and journalists noticed the musician's difficulties in moving on stage.
In mid-November, his relatives even had to deny a new internment.
Euro-2009/Short swimming pool: Bousquet's first title in his favourite distance
Frenchman Frédérick Bousquet, a triple medalist in the World Cup, won Thursday an international title in a distance for which he feels particular affection, 50 m free, during Euro-2009 in a short swimming swimming swimming pool in Istanbul.
On the first day of this competition in which polyurethane swimming swimmers were allowed for the last time, only three world records were surpassed when many more were actually expected.
