Pity for Bousquet, who coveted the world's record at 50m free, a brand he owns in a long swimming swimming pool (20.94).
In one of the two queen distances of swimming, in 50 m free, the 28-year-old Frenchman prevailed with 20'53 during a final where ten swimmers competed instead of eight as usual, a scoop at the international level.
Bousquet went to Croatian Duja Draganja (20.70) and Russian Sergey Fesikov (20.84), but did not manage to lower the world mark (20.30), as he had planned.
We can always aspire to something better and, in that case, I've already said that if the world record was within my reach, I was going to try to achieve it.
Unlike Bousquet, almost all swimmers wore polyurethane swimming swimmingboards starting on January 1 will be banned.
These swimming swimming swimsuits, originated from 238 world records since February 2008, ended their epic in Istanbul, where they first competed in 1999 for the same test.
This time the swimming swimming swimming wreck didn't help German Paul Biedermann, a long-swimming world champion this summer this summer in Rome.
The young swimming swimmer failed to lower his mark by 400 m free (3:32.77), however, he prevailed with the second best time of his career (3:34.55), against Russian Nikita Lobintsev (3:35.75) and Danish Mads Glaesner (3:36.82).
I didn't turn myself fully in this race.
I knew I wouldn't be gonna be able to break a world record, but I managed to defend my title and I fulfilled my mission.
Lobintsev will be a tough opponent in the future, Biedermann explained.
U.S. sarcophagus panel that belonged to Zola sold at $US1.5 million
A panel of Roman sarcophagus belonging to French writer Emile Zola was sold at $1.5 million on Thursday, well above the estimated price, Sotheby's art dealer announced.
This two-metre-long by 63-billimetre-tall panel dates back to the 3rd century after Christ, and was priced between $150,000 and $250,000.
The marble "representing four Dionymous scenes, in a sophisticated architectural framework, surrounded by satires and drunk women, is an uncommon piece, since there are only four or five in the world," Florent Heintz, vice-president of art auctions of ancient Rome and Egypt in Sotheby's, told AFP on Tuesday.
Sold between a hundred batches.
The sale continues, but until now, this lot has been the hardest run by the shoppers, without a doubt, a spokesman for Sotheby's, Dan Abernethy, told the AFP.
Just a few days ago the marble was discovered to be an acquisition of Emile Zola.
For almost 300 years, the panel was part of the famous Borges family collection in Rome, then appeared successively at the home of the French actress Cécile Sorel, as an element of his bathtub in his private Parisian mansion, and in the house of Paul Raynaud, minister of the Third French Republic and president of the Council for a short time, before Marshal Pétain took power.
For about fifty years, his whereabouts were uncertain.
Looking at the Louvre Museum database, Florent Heintz discovered that this panel was part of an " Emile Zola succession" opened in 1903, a year after the death of the author of "Therose Raquin" and the fresco of the "Rougon-Macquart."
Honduras Aborted Zelaya Exit to Mexico
On Thursday, Mexico estimated that the conditions could not be given to transfer to its territory the ousted president of Honduras, Manuel Zelaya, who was denied by the coup government.
At the moment, after a telephone conversation at night with President Zelaya, everything seems to indicate that there is no possibility of carrying out the transfer, Mexican Foreign Minister Patricia Espinosa said.
The plane of the Mexican government that arrived in El Salvador on Wednesday to take Zelaya returned to its place of origin, according to a source from the General Directorate of Migration.
In order to grant him safe passage, the coup government wanted to force Zelaya to "relinquish" and to seek political asylum abroad.
But the ousted president refused because he wanted to arrive in Mexico as head of state in invited office.
The coup government "suffered a new defeat by asking me to relinquish my office (...), they wanted me to resign," Zelaya told Globo radio on Thursday, in which he usually makes statements since the beginning of the political crisis that has been in Central America for five months.
"Here I can stay ten years, I've got my guitar," the deposed president, a refugee at Tegucigalpa's Brazilian embassy for two-and-a-half months.
Mexico's embassy submitted an application for safe conduct, but unfortunately we couldn't accept it because we couldn't contain a request for asylum, the interior minister of the embarrassment government, Oscar Raúl Matute, told Honduran radio HRN on Wednesday.
"I won't seek asylum from any country," Manuel Zelaya replied in an interview on Telesur, whose headquarters are in Caracas.
"If I leave Honduras, I'll do it as president of the Honduran people," he stressed.
Rumors about Zelaya's departure on Wednesday mobilised supporters of the deposed president, who rallied around Brazil's embassy, besieged heavily by the U.S.
Brazil's Foreign Minister Celso Amorim criticised the coup government's position.
"That reflects the fringe nature of this government in the face of international standards," he said.
Zelaya, who had approached Venezuela's socialist president, Hugo Chavez, was overthrown on June 28, when he had organized a referendum to prepare for his re-election, rejected by the Supreme Court, the army and Congress.
Since his exile in Costa Rica, he returned surprisingly on September 21 and took refuge at Brazil's embassy.
"If you leave, you will be arrested and you will have to answer to justice for the crime of "high betrayal," according to the coup government.
De facto President Roberto Micheletti managed to organize a presidential election on November 29 to replace Zelaya.
Elected Porfirio Lobo came out in the contentious scrutiny. This election was recognized by the United States, but questioned by Latin American countries, such as Venezuela, Argentina and Brazil.
Three days later, congress massively voted against Zelaya's return to power until the end of his term on January 27.
Wall Street Rises, U.S. Trade Scale Fuels Optimism
The New York Stock Exchange closed upwards on Thursday, U.S. trade balance figures faithfully witnessed the economic revival of both the United States and its partners: the Dow Jones gained 0.67 per cent; and the Nasdaq, 0.33 per cent.
