The agents forcibly boarded the dissident dissociers who were booed by supporters of the government, according to that journalist.
In general, dissatisfying dissatisfyments in similar circumstances only last a few hours.
"Ladies de Blanco," awarded the 2005 Sakharov Prize awarded by the European Parliament, had organized an unauthorized march to "request the release of all political prisoners," about 200 according to the dissent, said one of its leaders, Laura Pollan, whose husband, Hector Maceda, is serving a sentence of 20 years in prison.
Maceda is part of the group of 75 Cuban dissidents jailed in March 2003, of whom 53 remain behind bars.
The Cuban authorities call opponents "mercenaries" or "agents" at the expense of their declared enemy, the United States.
Unravelly in the media, Cuban television reported Thursday's demonstration by government militants against the "counter-revolutionaries."
Cuba, governed by a single party, the Communist Party, is constantly accused by European or American NGOs of trampling on rights and freedoms, especially those of expression.
An anglined shoots an octogenarian with a knife and leaves 5 injured in Clichy-la-Garenne
An unresponsive man with two knives killed an 80-year-old man and injuring five other people in the city center of Clichy-la-Garenne (High of the Seine) on Thursday at the time of greatest influx of the afternoon
According to the first elements of the investigation, the 28-year-old aggressor allegedly beat with two knives and, for reasons not yet determined, an old man, in the pharmacy or in front of the pharmacy at No. 85 on Jean-Jaura boulevard, police sources and witnesses reported.
As a result of the serious injuries, the man born in 1929 died in the hospital, according to a head of the Alliance police union.
Later in the pharmacy or in front of the pharmacy, he attacked a pregnant woman who was with her partner.
Husband in his thirties got in the way and was injured.
