He was "in a state of overexcitement" as "any deranged of this kind whose forces increase considerably under the effect of the action."
U.S.: Polanski's lawyer again asks to dismiss the case
Franco-Polish filmmaker Roman Polanski refuted this Thursday in Los Angeles (California, the West of the United States), to dismiss the case against his client, who is under house arrest in Switzerland for a case of sexual abuse that occurred more than 30 years ago.
Chad Hummel, a lawyer for "El Pianista," denounced before three judges of the Second District of California Court of Appeals for serious procedural errors at the time of the events, in 1977.
Hummel said: "I demand that the charges be dismissed at this very moment, as the conduct of the judge in charge of the case at the time, Laurence Rittenband, "was chilling."
According to him, Judge Rittenband (died since then) allegedly talked to a prosecutor who would have told him that Roman Polanski deserved to go to prison, before issuing a sentence, which constitutes a serious procedural misdemeanour.
This conversation was made public in the documentary "Roman Polanski: wanted and desired" (Roman Polanski, searched and desired), from the very mouth of the prosecutor in question, David Wells.
But Wells finally declared at the end of September "to have lied" to the author of the documentary.
Hummel called for a trial that would allow him to present the brilliance of his argument in the event that the court rejects the dismissal of the case.
The accusation, for its part, reiterated the argument set out in the first instance, i.e. that no dismissal of the charges could be seriously assessed until Roman Polanski appeared in court.
The 76-year-old filmmaker escaped from the United States in 1978 before his sentence was handed down for "illegal sexual relations" with a girl under the age of 13.
Since then, the United States has never stepped on again.
Los Angeles Deputy District Attorney Phyllis Asayama said that Roman Polanski "must be here to show up at the hearing."
"With this cause, do we want to convey the message not only to (Roman Polanski) but also to other defendants that there is the possibility of fleeing?" Asayama asked.
For her, the filmmaker "requests a favor from the court, although he despises his authority."
One of the three judges, who now have 90 days to deliver his sentence, also stressed "that there were other alternatives to the decision to flee."
Hummel wasn't the only one seeking to dismiss the case against Roman Polanski.
Samantha Geimer &apos; s attorney, the victim, also once again claimed to close the case.
He argues that his client can no longer support being harassed every time the Polanski case resonates, adding: "No one in this room can say the procedure was fair.
Twenty-two, that's enough.
Roman Polanski was arrested September 26 in Switzerland on US arrest warrant.
After more than two months behind bars, he was sentenced to a home prison in his Gstaad hut, pending an eventual extradition.
Wall Street loses its course and squeezes through the rise of the dollar
The New York Stock Exchange closed this Friday aimlessly, in a market that is being debated between indicators better than those predicted in the United States and the rise of the dollar: the Dow Jones gained 0.63 per cent, but the Nasdaq lost 0.03 per cent.
According to the definitive figures at the close, the Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 65.67 points to 10,471.50 points, while Nasdaq of technological component lost 0.55 points to 2,190.31 points.
The broader Standard & Poor's 500 index advanced 0.37% (4.06 points), reaching 1.106.41 points.
Honestly, the market is very quiet, said Mace Bwsilver of Marblehead Asset Management.
Stay with narrow margins, the volume of exchanges is shrinking and I think it's going to stay that way until the end of the year.
