The Election of Miss World Again in South Africa
After a month-to-to-tour of South-South Africa, Miss World's title candidates are in the final line to fulfill their dream: to be chosen the most beautiful woman in the world compared to a billion spectators.
During this great celebration of the beauty organized for the second consecutive year around Johannesburg, five of them have already achieved their participation in the semi-final.
Miss Japan Eruza Sasaki, Perla Beltran of Mexico, Mariatu Kargbo of Sierra Leone, Kaiane Aldorino de Gibraltar and Yanbing Ma of Canada were chosen Miss Athlete or even Miss Top Model during her stay in Africa.
It was a real scramble.
It was extraordinary to visit beautiful cities such as Port Elizabeth, Durban and Cape Town.
"I saw a lot of things and I experienced countless experiences," Miss Sierra Leone said astonished.
For the nineteen-time competition in South Africa, will be broadcast live Saturday from Midrand, north of Johannesburg, starting at 5 p.m. (15 h GMT).
Impossible to imagine a better country to deliver my wreath.
This experience was fascinating.
"I'm so glad I've loved it twice," says Miss World Ksenia Sukhinova in the southern summer heat.
For the young Russian, his second stay in South Africa was marked by numerous exchanges with the candidates and the possibility of rediscovering "African landscapes that take their breath away."
After a month of galas, rehearsals, press conferences and safaris, the candidates for Miss World are concentrated in the competition.
Indian Pooja Chopra hopes to be able to follow in the footsteps of Priyanka Chopra, winning in 2000 and present in this election, as well as Aishwarya Rai in 1994, both becoming Bollywood stars.
Every woman &apos; s dream is to be crowned the most beautiful in the world, but it's not just about appearance.
"We are the ambassadors of our countries, and I want to return to my country with the wreath," says Miss India with a big smile.
According to the organizers, music and dancing will mark this 59th edition.
Candidates will parade in traditional dresses and, of course, swimming.
Chinese presenter Angela Chow, former South African Beauty queen Michelle McLean, and Miss World committee manager Steve Douglas will lead the event.
South Africa proved to be able to receive the world with warmth and pride difficult to match, says Julia Morley, president of the Miss World contest.
Like last last year, the candidates made a safari, visited Soweto's celebrity, but this time cycling, and tasted the local cuisine.
As an exceptional fact, they knew the South African president, Jacob Zuma, who shook their hands.
