News of Johnny Hallyday "is very positive," according to his son David
The news about Johnny Hallyday "were very positive," his son David said on Friday when he arrived at the Los Angeles airport to accompany his father hospitalized for several days.
The news is very positive.
"Dad is strong and everything is doing very well," David Hallyday told the press as he got off the plane that brought him from Paris shortly after 1 p.m. local (21 h GMT).
The singer &apos; squeezed son said he would move "as soon as possible" to the Cedars-Sina hospital in Los Angeles, where Johnny Hallyday was admitted on Monday due to an infection resulting from the discal hernia operation on November 26 in Paris.
The musician is "in an induced coma from that night in Los Angeles for reasons of comfort, in order to prevent him from suffocating and thus being able to administer his treatment," the press service said.
Johnny Hallyday had already been subjected to an induced coma by the doctors at the Cedar-Sina hospital in Los Angeles, where he was admitted on Monday for an infection, a sequel to a discal hernia operation on November 26 last in Paris.
The singer underwent surgery once again the night from Wednesday to Thursday due to serious injuries caused by the previous intervention.
On Friday morning his wife Laeticia went to the French lycée of Los Angeles, attended by his daughter Jade, to witness a school event, an AFP photographer confirmed.
The Head of Orthopaedic Surgery and Traumatology of the Pitié-Salpere Hospital, Yves Catoné, announced that he would travel on Monday in Los Angeles to be with the singer.
The producer violently accused Dr. Stéphane Delajoux, the neurosurgeon who operated the singer on November 26 at the international private clinic of Monceau Park in Paris.
We've been told it was a massacre.
Surgeons in L.A. said they were outraged, Camus said.
There's talk of a possible lawsuit, he said.
Jean-Claude Camus said Johnny Hallyday had "traveled too quickly to Los Angeles with the permission of the Parisian surgeon."
"I spoke directly to the doctor and I was shocked when he assured me that there was no problem making the trip, as I was operated on twice as a discal hernia," he added.
Shortly before at RTL station, he indicated that the singer had apparently "been without a candle, without anything," after his Parisian operation.
Dr. Stéphane Delajoux is well-known in the artistic environment.
She operated on actress Marie Trintignant in Vilnius a few days before her death at a Neuilly-sur-Seine clinic where Delajoux was working at the time.
In 2007, the Charlotte Gainsbourg actress also intervened to reabsorb a bruise formed by a brain haemorrhage.
He is a reputable doctor, convicted by justice on several occasions of malpractice, as well with tax fraud and swindling.
Olivier Metzner, a lawyer for the Order of Physicians, accused him of being "an unscrupulous man" suffocated "regularly" before the Order's Council, and is "left away from being renowned."
After several days of silence, Dr. Stéphane Delajoux spoke Friday through his lawyer, David Koubbi.
He indicated that the intervention "was carried out without any inconvenience" and that the results of the postoperative studies were "normal."
The operation "couldn't cause wreaths and therefore I didn't need drainage," he added.
Either way, the tour of the singer, whose health problems are multiplying since the summer, went into a parenthesis.
"Undoubtedly, resuming the to tour on January 8 (in Amiens, NDLR) as planned seems very risky to me," Camus said.
The UDF association raises its tone and warns the New Center about the use of its acronym
The association UDF (Union for French Democracy), controlled by the MoDem (Democratic Movement) of Francois Bayrou, reacted this Friday to the attempt of the President of the New Center, Hervé Morin, to launch an OPA (Public Acquisition offer) on the acronym of the former giscardia party, intimidating him to withdraw it from all support, on pain of being sued.
Hervé Morin publicly announced his intention to retrieve the acronym UDF, as he considers that Francois Bayrou was not in a position to defend his political heritage after reaching his hand to the left.
On the other hand, the Minister of Defense, whose party is associated with the UMP, had planned to confirm the strategy on Saturday before the NC National Council.
In support of his management, he received the help of Congressman Hervé de Charette (former UMP, current NC) who claims the ownership of the UDF brand, since he was the first to register it at the National Institute of Industrial Property in 2004.
But on Friday night, the office of the UDF association, controlled by the friends of Francois Bayrou who do not want to be deprived of it, broke the silence to issue a request to the Minister and the deputy by intimidating the acronym and to transfer the property rights of the trademark to them, under penalty of judicial demand.
These last few days, you have made many statements insinuating that you were going to propose to your political movement to retrieve the acronym UDF in a letter signed by 19 of the 24 members of the office, including Francois Bayrou and Marielle de Sarnez del MoDem, as well as Senator Jean Athuis of the Centrist Alliance.
They also believe it is right to continue with the use of the name of the movement, the "Nouveau centre," and the slogan "l'UDF d'aujourd'hui" in its militant material and on the home page of its site, the signatories of the letter say.
As they already know, they have no right to use the acronym, explains the UDF office, which demands that the NC withdraw "immediately the mention of the name of the UDF of all support, as well as (its) Internet site."
It also asks Hervé de Charette "to transfer the ownership of (the) brand to the legitimate beneficiaries."
Failure to do so, justice will be sought to enforce the law and the legitimate rights of members of the UDF, the signatories warn.
In the letter, they specify that the UDF "is a true political movement since 1978" and recall that Morin and his friends "eliged" leaving in 2007 "to create a new party, as others had already done in 2002 to create the UMP."
Therefore, the New Center does not, according to them, have any right to use the acronym UDF.
They also recall that the last congress of the UDF voted in 2007 to accede to the MoDem for three years and the creation of an office in charge of defending "the material and moral interests" of the movement founded by Valéry Giscard d'Estaing.
As for Hervé de Charette, the office continues, "when he registered the mark," he was no longer "a member of the UDF for three years," but of the UMP.
Outrageous and fiercely take advantage of the absence of acronym UDF registration to carry it out on its own.
This indiscretion is being transformed into a crime, they denounce, before mocking the NC's strategy: "We believe that in politics, as in life, it is more worthy to make its own name rather than trying to leave the anonymity by falsifying a name that isn't entitled."
Liliane Bettencourt case: Francois-Marie Banier will be tried for abuse of weakness
This on Friday the Court of Nanterre decided to put the artist Francois-Marie Banier on trial to determine whether the daughter of billionaire Liliane Bettencourt claims, he took advantage of the state of weakness of the octogenarian to plunder her of a part of her fortune.
The 62-year-year-old photographer, known for pictures of the show's stars, will stand trial on April 15 and 16, 2010 for abuse of weakness, an offence whose conviction consists of a maximum sentence of three years in prison and 375,000 euros in fine.
This means that in this case there will be a process worthy of a saga about one of the richest families in France, in which mother-d-daughter disputes the reins of the L'Oréal cosmetics empire.
But Correctional Court No.15 also ordered 87-year-old Liliane Bettencourt to undergo a medical examination by three medical professionals.
The heiress and main shareholder of L'Oréal has always refused to undergo such examinations, even further under the conditions proposed by the Nanterre Prosecutor's Office during the investigation.
Physicians appointed this Friday will have to submit a report by 10 March 2010 stating whether Liliane Bettencourt suffered states of weakness.
I think he's gonna accept.
"I am going to talk to her," Ms. Bettencourt &apos; s attorney, Georges Kiejman, said when he left the hearing.
For his part, the lawyer of Francoise Bettencourt-Meyers, Olivier Metzner, was pleased with the court &apos; s decision.
"Excellent news (...) The expertise will show that he (Banier) manipulated this elder lady, whom we must protect, she said.
For the past two years, Liliane Bettencourt &apos; s only daughter initiated criminal proceedings against Francois-Marie Banier, a multi-faceted photographer and artist, accusing him of taking advantage for years of his mother's weakness to obtain one billion euros in donations, through cheques, life insurance contracts or works of art.
Liliane Bettencourt, for his part, has always denied having been manipulated by the photographer, whom he considers a long-standing friend.
On Friday, while the court was examining the provenance of Ms Bettencourt-Meyers &apos; stabs, the lawyers &apos; pleads were sometimes a large family confession.
Liliane Bettencourt &apos; s lawyer therefore took into account three letters her client sent (in 1999, 2003 and 2005) to her notary stating that she distrusted Francoise Bettencourt-Meyers &apos; husband.
In the 2003 letter, Liliane Bettencourt added, according to the lawyer: "I could explain to my daughter that I wrote to Banier between once and twice a day for fifteen years.
Since his wedding, I couldn't talk to Francoise anymore."
For its part, the Nanterre Public Prosecutor &apos; s Office once again considered that the plaintiff was not in a position to appear, all the more so since a guardianship judge, in the last few days, refused to proceed with her request to open a judicial guardianship procedure for her mother.
Liliane Bettencourt "has the full capacity to be brought to justice, she is not under guardianship and does not have a guardian.
Neither other person will be able to appear in his place," said the representative of the Public Prosecutor &apos; s Office, Marie-Chrsitine Daubigney.
Ultimately, the court decided that it would rule after the judgement on the admissibility of Mrs Bettencourt-Meyers.
Managing revolution or end of business intelligence?
"If nothing changes in the management of the great French companies, our struggle for the development of business intelligence will have been in vain...
More than ten years ago, the attitude of the management of the big companies towards their earnhires is reminiscent of the position of the companies towards consumers before the marketing revolution: it is that the collaborators adhere to "values" determined from the top as previously it was about imposing on consumers products designed by factory technicians.
By putting the client at the center of the company, the marketing revolution gave its place to the strategies to conquer the markets... although the authoritarian temptation to impose products that are not related to customer preference factors is repeated regularly.
Demobilization of employees may end in deadly demoralization.
Suicides at France Telecom, PSA or Renault are just the tip of the iceberg of a much more general phenomenon that particularly affects executives.
This demobilization seriously compromises the development of all the intellectual functions of the company, especially R &amp; R &amp; R &apos; s and, moreover, business intelligence.
Indeed, the BI (Business Intelligence) "requires that all the parties involved (...) have proactive conduct and a forward-looking view"; it needs a commitment on the part of the employees that completely opposes the recommendations of an authoritarian management.
For this reason, we call for a managerial revolution, which would consist of putting the collaborators at the centre of the company's worries as well as the Customer.
The internal opinion polls have a relative utility
Will internal opinion polls, such as Orange &apos; s questionnaire among its wage earners, encourage the managerial revolution in practice?
Unfortunately, I'm afraid I don't.
"In principle, because I was able to verify that, in the face of a new situation, and after a lot of twists, all the companies end up doing essentially... what they've always done B989
- Secondly, because authoritarianism, one of the pathologies of power, grows proportionally to the importance of its own failures.
"Just because most managers have a simplistic view of human behavior.
Because they come from the great schools of engineers, they interpret psychology in a mechanistic way.
They adopt, by intellectual comfort, the ancient behavioral theory that American "neurosciences" recently revived, and that the Institutes of Studies and the media spread widely behind their backs.
In this way, by suggesting that it is possible to handle "commands" (sic) of the commitment of the executives, a recent Ipsos investigation published on Le Figaro.fr reaffirms the directors in a mechanistic management position, unfazed to today's world.
Identifying and measuring mobilisation factors
On the contrary, the managerial revolution necessary for the proper functioning of modern companies implies adopting a finalist vision of human behavior.
It consists of integrating into every reasoning or practice of business management, a fundamental fact: for wage earners, commitment to the company is only possible if it clearly appears as a means to realize part of their dreams, ambitions and projects.
Nowadays, only studies emanating from Mustifunctional Analyst adopt this conception of the motivations of employees.
The studies have shown that they judge their company on the basis of a predetermined doxa that allows them, through the image they have of the company, to evaluate what it offers them to achieve their own life projects.
Thanks to the identification and measurement of mobilization factors, company managers can control, foresee and anticipate their "social performance" and relocate human resource management "on the site."
In this way, they create a favorable terrain for the organized development of strong and effective business intelligence.
Absul speech: "Just war" Obama gets praise from the right
On Friday, the U.S. right, satisfied that the 2009 Nobel Peace Laureate has defended the concept of "fair war" against the enemies of the U.S., acclaimed Obama's speech on Thursday.
Supporters, who had disallowed the Obama award on October 9, this time applauded the presidential speech, as did Sarah Palin, a former vice-presidential nominating candidate during the 2008 elections.
"She loved what she said," said Ms. Palin, a leading figure on the right wing of the Republican party to the USA Today newspaper, adding that she herself had addressed the issue of war "sometimes necessary" in her newly published memoir.
In my opinion, the war is, of course, the last thing the Americans want to be forced to do, but it is necessary.
"The terrorists must be arrested," he said.
Mr. Obama, who inherited the two wars of his predecessor George W. Bush in Iraq and in Afghanistan, dedicated himself, this Thursday in Oslo, to justify the use of the force, a way to explain his decision to send 30,000 more troops to Afghanistan nine days earlier.
Another opponent of Obama, Newt Gingrich, considered that the president had done "a very good job" in Oslo.
"I think that the speech was really good," rejoiced the former Speaker of the House of Representatives, who noted in particular the mention of "the existence of evil in the world" in the presidential words.
"At a time, the speech seemed very historic," Gingrich said during an interview on the radio show "The Takeaway."
The former representative-elect, who some see as a possible Republican inauguration nominee for the 2012 presidential elections, was pleased to listen to "a left-wing president" when he reminded members of the Nobel Committee "that they wouldn't be free and they couldn't deliver a peace prize if the use of force didn't exist."
According to Michael Gerson, the author of the George W. Bush speeches and current chronicler in the Washington Post, Obama delivered "a very American speech."
In addition to presenting himself as a "citizen of the world," as "always he does," he "spoke as an American president" by reminding Europeans "who live in a security bubble that they didn't create and who don't do much to preserve it," Gerson said.
Among the unconvincing right-wing, a Bush-era hawk, former U.N. Ambassador John Bolton described the speech as "monotonous, pretentious and uninspired" on the National Review Online website.
To the president's left, Democratic Representative Dennis Kucinich was alarmed to see Barack Obama justifying the use of force.
"When we cover the war with the notion of righteousness, it's easy to justify the massacre of innocent people," he warned.
The centre-left press, however, praised the president.
Los Angeles Time, while still believing that Obama received the Nobel laureate prematurely, described his speech as "super-production" especially for a speaker known for his talent.
Oslo's discourse "should serve as a roadmap to guide international decisions in the fight against conflicts, poverty and oppression," the Californian newspaper enthusiastically said.
Obama "pronounced the speech that was necessary," the New York Times said, judging "while bleak and edifying."
Road transport: three marathon negotiations to ensure Christmas supply
After three marathon negotiating sessions, the unions in the road sector and the TLF (Transport and Logistics of France), the only employer organization that remained until the end, arrived on Friday afternoon at an agreement that puts an end to the strike slogan and the threat that falls to the Christmas supply.
During the signing session on Friday night, the Secretary of State of Transport, Dominique Bussereau, was "rejoiced" by the agreement, after several negotiating sessions that began on Wednesday morning.
After a second night of discussions, the negotiators said goodbye at dawn on Friday in the hope of quickly signing the settlement text late in the morning.
Although three employers' organizations (FNTR, Unostra, OTRE) withdrew slapping.
But a meeting of more than six hours that began at 11 a.m. was necessary for everyone to agree and the text will be signed at 6 p.m.
The conflict "would have been very harmful to companies" and "it was feared to disturb the end-of-year holidays," Bussereau said.
The text foresees an increase of 4 per cent for the lowest wages (in local and regional road transport), and 2.9 per cent for the highest (international transport).
The hourly rate rises from 8.72 euros to 9.06 for low wages.
"It's not very much, but we started with a lot of disadvantage," explained Patrice Clos (FO).
If so, we're going to give the order to our militants that there'll be no more cuts or blockades in the supply centers.
"But there can always be some dissatisfaction," he added.
Philippe Grillot, president of the TLF, acknowledged that it is "a good agreement," although it was "difficult to grant increases to high wages taking into account international competition."
According to him, the increments will be in the order of 170 euros per month for low wages and from 100 to 120 euros for the highest.
"It's a common-sense agreement that respects wage earners," said Maxime Dumont (CFDT), while Thierry Douine (CFTC) recognized it as "a historic agreement."
For Richard Jaubert (CGT) it is "an acceptable commitment."
The agreement also envisages an increase in travel costs of 3 per cent and the holding of general assemblies of road transport in the first half of 2010, which will mainly make it possible to address the issue of extraordinary pay and a modernization of the collective agreement.
Bussereau recalled that "a decrease of 100 million euros in bosses" was the subject of an amendment by the government in the 2009 rectification finance bill, which the National Assembly voted on Thursday night.
The government will ask the Senate to vote "under the same terms."
TLF &apos; s signature allows the agreement to be applied, in the first place, to the companies adhering to this organisation and, secondly, to the entire profession, in the event that the Minister of Labour makes an extension.
It'll be done "as soon as possible," Bussereau said.
Opponents of bosses could only delay the process.
The FNTR denounced that it was an "negotiated business," as the 100 million euros "had already been pledged to the sector to compensate for some of the CO2 tax."
The Secretary of State for Transport obliged us to negotiate in an urgent situation, not as a conclusion to an analysis discussed by the parties, but not to contradict those who want to eat turkey at the upcoming Christmas Eve dinner mourned.
For the OTRE, the agreement threatens "the stability of so many SMEs in the sector over the next 12 months."
RER A: Clashes Over the Weekend, Trade Union Disappointed With RATP Proposals
Controversies at RER A, France's largest urban public transport line, are set to continue over the weekend, because at Friday's meeting, the two-day strike between trade unions and RATP's management, there was no agreement and the unions were dissatisfied with regard to their premium request.
For this important weekend of Christmas shopping, on Saturday and Sunday only trains will circulate between 10 a.m. and 8 p.m., due to a train of every two on the central section (between La Défense and Nation) and one train of every four on the other lines.
Interconnection with the SNFC will be suspended in Nanterre Préfecture, which will force passengers to change train.
The Lafayette Galleries, which are expecting 200,000 customers every day, were glad that "the RATP strives to provide service to the big stores."
The RER A crosses the Parisian city from the west to the east.
The RATP section runs from Saint-Germain-en-Laye and Nanterre to Boissy-Saint-Léger and Marne-La-Vallée.
This on Friday, approximately one in two trains circulated at rush hours, and almost no train during valley hours, as had been expected.
On Thursday there was no mess on the platforms thanks to the organization of the inhabitants of the Isle of France.
In this way at the dawn, Saint-Germain-en-Laye station (Yvelines) was much less crowded than usual, especially due to the absence of students.
On Boissy-Saint-Léger (Val-de-Marne), Jack Nbakina, a 29-year-year-old engineer, explained that he had risen "an hour and a half earlier to get to schedule."
Saint-Lazare station did not swear, said SNCF, which had provided for the supervision of police and law enforcement.
RER A operated by the SNCF from Cergy-Le-Haut arrived or departed from Saint-Lazare's station to unite metres.
New general assemblies of striking workers will take place on Saturday and Sunday. The results will be analyzed, "unsatisfyingly," according to the unions, of a meeting that took place with the management on Friday afternoon.
The director of the RERs proposes to negotiate a specific protocol in all respects for RER A: working time, professional development, etc.
That's not what the drivers who claim a premium that's owed them want, according to Thierry Garon (Sud).
The RATP even puts its premium proposals in parentheses in parentheses, the CGT said, noting that management "was not able to solve a problem that the SNCF and the road sector had already solved."
A new meeting is scheduled on Monday to carry out "a more extensive analysis of the conditions of the exercise of the profession," the RATP said.
Which also led the unions to say the company "didn't care about weekend users."
In the morning, about 150 drivers invaded a committee at headquarters in Paris.
The management, which until then refused to talk during the strike, then suggested the meeting "to soothe the waters."
Controversy could be extended to RER B on Tuesday, after Unsa and the Independants have issued a notice.
The trade unions request a premium of 120 euros (Unsa) to 150 euros, of which 30 euros are variable (CGT, FO, CFDT, Sud, Indépendants) and complain about the deterioration of working conditions on this line that has one million passengers per day.
RER drivers, all in the second-degree of their professional racing, charge 2,200 to 2,700 gross euros and 600 to 650 euros of bonuses, for hours with alternating shifts, Sundays and Holidays.
Turkish justice dissolves the pro-kurdo party, a decision with serious consequences
Turkey's Constitutional Court ruled Friday in favor of the dissolution of the country's main pro-Kurdish party, a decision that immediately provoked demonstrations of anger in the Kurdish community in the south-east of the country and in Istanbul.
This decision could greatly complicate the work of the government, which has just reached out to the Kurdish community trying to put an end to 25 years of conflict.
The Party of the Democratic Society (DTP) was dissolved because it had become the "focus of activities that harmed the independence of the state and its indivisible unity," the President of the Court, Hasim Kili, told the press after four days of deliberations.
The decision of the 11 judges was unanimous, although only a seven-vote majority was needed to obtain the dissolution, he said, adding that 37 of the leaders of the party, among whom are its president Ahmet Tirk and Congresswowoman Aysel Tugluk, are removed from political life for five years.
In addition, the judge announced the cessation of the parliamentary immunity enjoyed by Tirk and Tugluk and the confiscation of the party's property by the Treasury.
The DTP has 21 seats in Parliament (of 550).
Its leaders warned ahead of the verdict that MPs would step down from parliament before being a non-partipartisan parliamentarians.
The decision stems as a result of a procedure launched by Supreme Court Prosecutor Abdurrahman Yalinkaya in 2007, which accuses the DTP of obeying the "principles" of the Kurdistan Workers' Party (PKK), regarded as a terrorist organization by Ankara and by many other countries.
Numerous observers believe the DTP is the PKK's legal political showcase.
However, the party claims that it does not have "organic links" with the PKK, but it refuses to describe it as a terrorist organization and calls on the government to negotiate with it.
The Court &apos; s verdict intervenes at a time when the government extends a hand to the Kurdish community proposing a series of measures to strengthen their rights, so that the Kurds stop supporting the PKK and thus put an end to the conflict.
DTP leaders stated that a dissolution could lead to a resurgence of tensions in South-east anatomium, where a large number of demonstrations against PKK boss Abdullah Ecalan degenerated into clashes with the police over the past few weeks.
Since the announcement of justice, thousands of protesters have rallied in front of DTP premises in Diyarbakir, South-East's great town populated mostly by Kurds.
Ordain forces threw tear grenades and used water canyons, while protesters threw Molotov bombs or smelling stones.
Approximately a hundred people were also concentrated in Istanbul.
Upon the verdict, Tirk stated that the decision of justice would "deerate discouragement."
"Turkey can't resolve this issue (kurda, NDLR) by dissolving a party, he told the press.
The government, made up of a party emanating from the Islamist movement, also laments the decision.
As a matter of principle, we are opposed to the closure of parties.
"This does not solve anything," said Energy Minister Taner Yildiz.
"This decision completely boycotts the democratic openness launched by the government, said political analyst Ahmet Insel.
"We can, of course, expect a reaction from the PKK," he added.
On Thursday, the PKK claimed an attack in which seven soldiers were killed three days earlier in northern Turkey, one of the bloodiest attacks in recent months.
The snow snowfall can suddenly start
A truly cooling winter is what Wolfgang Brauburger wants.
It's not at all something you want to make life impossible for citizens, says FES' winter road maintenance director
But with that its employees can learn to test and dominate the totality of vehicle technology.
Metecologists may be announced for the first time at the weekend.
More than 330 employees and 120 vehicles are poised to handle snow and ice.
In this case the FES acts in accordance with a thorough plan.
As soon as it sweeps profusely in Frankfurt and temperatures fall below zero degrees, there comes 20 of the so-called A stretches with large vehicles with salt, Brauburger explains.
Main arteries and avenues such as Neue Weg and the Hanauer Landstras are included, for example.
But there are also accesses for the fire brigade, public transport routes, pedestrian areas and sloping sections, such as the Atzelbergstrasse.
FES service kicks off at around 3 a.m.
Approximately 1,040 kilometers should be covered, if necessary, for a maximum of three hours with wet salt, which adheres better.
That's why the FES begins at around three o'clock in the morning, "because the lanes are free until rush hour traffic begins."
It's the tough time it starts snowing around 7:30 a.m.
"So we've got a problem," says the director of road maintenance in winter, "because then no one's ready to clear space for vehicles with salt."
Immediately afterwards, when it is no longer snowing, it's up to the secondary and private roads, to which the Rat-Beil-Stra'e also belongs.
The last priority is the lateral and secondary streets.
That's where the inconvenience is generated, Brauburger knows.
At about five o'clock in the in the morning the infantry of the FES is launched, the dangerous points and cruise ships are mainly cleared.
Usually the FES couldn't take care of all bike lanes.
In Frankfurt there is a special service, which from 22 to 4:30 a.m. doesn't lose sight mainly of the bridges, Brauburger says.
As these quickly slippery, the salt-fired vehicle gets started as soon as the temperature drops below two degrees.
In the event of an emergency, the police can also call them.
The dinosaur cradle
The Tawa finding looked the same as many other carnivorous dinosaurs: a two-metre-long, 1.50-metre-high animal that ran on the two strong hind legs, had an elongated head with sharp and curved teeth, short arms with sickle-shaped claws and a long tail.
If the species had lived a couple of million years later, it wouldn't have been something special.
But the Tawa found was one of the first dinosaurs with such bodily features, reports Sterling Nesbitt from the University of Texas at Austin and colleagues in Thursday's journal Science.
The species lived about 215 million years ago in what is now New Mexico, which at that time was located in a dry area of the supercontinent Pangea on the equator.
Tawa hatched a new twist towards the early evolution of dinosaurs, Nesbitt said.
It shows us a lot about genealogical relationships, the propagation and characteristics of the early dinosaurs.
Nesbitt and other paleontologists began an excavation at Ghost Ranch in northern New Mexico in 2006.
"Sometimes we've found a dozen dinosaur bones in one day, which is unprecedented for Triassic Sedies," says Randall Irmis, a team member of the Utah Museum of Natural History team.
Investigators quickly identified some bones as belonging to a new species.
During the excavation a total they collected the remains of five specimens.
A finding confirms the theories about ancient dinosaur history
The name "Tawa" comes from the sun god of the Hopi Indians.
For Sterling Nesbitt is an exceptional finding: "Dinosaur Fossils of the Triassic are extremely rare, usually only individual fragments are found."
Other dinosaur pundits don't fully share such enthusiasm.
This finding is not particularly spectacular, but it confirms some theories about the ancient history of dinosaurs, says Martin Sander of the University of Bonn.
According to today's commute, dinosaurs first turned up about 230 million years ago.
But many of the oldest fossils are incomplete, so the ranking of these early dinosaurs is quite controversial.
The experts are therefore debating, for example, when saurians were divided into their three most important genealogical lineages: carnivorous theropods including Tyrannosaurus Rex, the Velociraptor and also the bird, quadriped sauropods known as "long collars" and their relatives, the herbívorous Ornithischia (saurs with bird pelvis), to which, for example, the Triceratops and Stegosaurus species belong.
According to analyzing Nesbitt and his colleagues, Tawa finds is a primitive theropod.
This one shows an interesting combination of primitive and progressive properties, Nesbitt says.
For example, in the Tawa skull, the researchers found clues of airbags, such as those also owned by the descendants of the theropods, the birds.
Thanks to airbags, bird breath is more efficient than mammals.
In Tawa's case you also find the bones of the spine were evidently filled with partial air.
Tawa's the theropod with older, primitive airbags, says Randall Irmis.
Because the related line of subsequent giant sauropods also possessed air bags and light bones, it was likely that the common ancestors of both groups would already produce this useful innovation.
At least three migratory waves into North America
Because the most primitive dinosaurs so far known lived in South America, the researchers deduce that saufers first sprouted there.
As a result, the three genealogical lineages were initially separated and stretched from South America through all corners of the Earth, which at that time still formed a unique land mass, the Pangea supercontinent.
However, the facts say the team found along with Tawa to find the remains of two other theropods not closely related.
"All three can't come down from a one-on-sympathizer, the researchers argue.
"We believe there were at least three migratory waves to North America," says Alan Turner of the American Museum of Natural History in New York, co-author of the Science study.
Naturally, there are opponents too.
An even older species, Silesaurus, which belongs to a sibling dinosaur group, lived in today &apos; sweeping Poland, Bonn paleontologist Martin Sander says.
His opinion could give a distorted image, because simply in South America many dinosaur fossils continue to be preserved, while on other continents there is barely anything to discover.
The question of how dinosaurs managed to evolve from a relatively weird collateral line from reptiles to Mesozoic dominant terrestrial vertebrates remains an enigma.
Meanwhile, in the Triassic dinosaurs still shared their living space with many other reptiles, for example the ancestors of current crocodiles and pterosaurs and with many other saurries.
Towards the end of the Triassic the dinosaurs spread all over the world and many new species were developed, while kinship became extinct at the Triassic boundary towards the Jurassic.
Happy histories weren't eternal
There's two hypotheses to explain this change, Martin Sander says.
It could have been given a mass extinction that dinosaurs survived by chance, but the other reptiles didn't.
Either dinosaurs were better-adapted, so they could beat opponents in the area.
Happy histories aren't always eternal.
150 million years later, a meteorite impact curbs the rise of a group of species, which had also already evolved in the Triassic and have since carried a miserable existence: mammals.
Solidarity From Vienna to Sao Paulo
The list is a long-distance and the international signatories.
Professors from the University of Toronto, Cambridge, Vienna or Berkeley in California participate.
Teachers from Naples, Québec, Edinburgh, New York, Sao Paulo, Berlin or Bremen participate.
The police evict from the busy casino from Frankfurt's Goethe University affected around the world.
278 professors and professors of Goethe University, although also from higher education centers throughout Germany, Europe and North and South America, sympathize with students in protest at the Campus of Westend.
