In the U.S. airstrike, requested by Kunduz's German commander of Kunduz, Colonel Klein, according to the report of the NATO investigation, killed or injured as many as 142 people were injured.
The Greens, the SPD and the left on Saturday urged Federal Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) to speak out immediately before the federal parliament and to clarify what Gutenberg really knew about the incident.
Mrs. Merkel needs to clarify whether an intentional assassination strategy is part of the federal government &apos; s policy in Afghanistan, and if the chancellor, army and the intelligence service have approved this new strategy, J.rgen Trittin and her Greens colleague Renate Konast demanded.
Government spokeswoman Ulrich Wilhelm strongly rejected the criticism.
The chancellor's office exerted no influence on concrete actions of the German army in Afghanistan, she said.
Doubts on Saturday had also raised doubts about the Ministry &apos; shouting the reasons for the sacking of Inspector-General Wolfgang Schneiderhan and Secretary of State Peter Wichert.
According to SPIEGEL information, both had informed Gutenberg correctly and completely about the background of the attack on the convoys.
Officially it had been said that they had denied the existence of the NATO reports and had hidden sensitive details from the minister.
However, from around the two high-ranking officials it transcended that they had even informed the Ministry of the Defence of a number of reports, including a two-sided document of Colonel Klein, who was in charge, and a field patrol report.
In the meantime, Wichert reportedly requested in a letter to clarify the matter, reports the newspaper "Frankfurter Allgemeiner Sonntagszeitung."
