The ministry did not demonstrate on Saturday regarding the rebukes made to it, and pointed out that this would be clarified in the commission of inquiry of the Federal Parliament, which will be constituted next Wednesday.
Thorical worlds
In a waking state, some people see images and hear voices that others don't see or hear.
Not only in the case of the mentally ill and adjudgments fade the boundaries between the imagination and the reality.
As long as it happens during the sleep, it's all in order.
"When we're lying in our obscure-eyed bedrooms, we can unimpedeously give the wildest imaginations and fantasy creatures.
However, whoever experiences sleep-like states during vigil, or sees and hears things that others don't see or hear, is viewed most of the time in the modern Western world as crazy or junkie, or at least as a maniac.
But such images and voices don't just arise in the mentally ill or drugged.
Our imagination lies: the same neural processes that allow us to design houses, paint pictures or write novels, at times cause the boundaries between sleep, madness and reality to blur.
Sierica-modified alert states of consciousness may appear in the most diverse forms, at best we call them visions, and at the Worst, insanity.
Although the untriyers and the effects can be distinguished, the visions of Hildegard von Bingen mystique can be referred to similar processes, the psychedelic experiences of the prophet of the New Era Carlos Castañeda, the states of shamanic possession, psychosis and hallucinations.
Hallucinations or deliriums arise when the balance of neuronal substances of communication in the brain - especially dopamine, noradrenaline, serotonin and acetylcholine have been disrupted and the interaction between various parts of it is disturbed.
Complex hallucinations and crazy thoughts appear during schizophrenic psychosis during schizophrenic psychosis.
Gravity no longer has power over him.
The mushroom-to-sleep relationship has occupied philosophers and doctors since ancient times.
Aristotle confided the hallucinations as a form of waking-swashing dream, Wilhelm Griesinger, one of the founders of modern psychiatric psychiatry, observed in 1861 an "great resemblance of the state of craziness with the dream states," for Sigmund Freud, the dream was even "a psychosis, with all the nonsense, crazy formations, and mirages from it."
It's Autumn in 2001, when Henning T. becomes a comic figure.
Its body is two-story and outrageously colourful.
Gravity doesn't have power over it anymore, it's hovering near the bedroom ceiling.
He gets kicked.
Behind him, demon-studded figures with wings, with red grins such as blood, deformed, gnanching their teeth.
They're chasing him, kicking him in heavy boots and flogging, they throw him against the walls, he senses the pains all over his body.
Strident and malicious punches resonate from the walls and the radius.
He can't stand them anymore, the voices that insult and offend him.
Henning T. boots two stereo cables, moans them to form a hanging knot, and sets them on a hook on the ceiling.
He gets in a horn and knots around his neck.
Jump.
String is loosened.
"Let it serve as a lesson," scream the voices in a choir that burst the eardrums, go to the clinic and make them heal you."
Now, Henning T. is in his 40s and has experienced six acute psychosis, the last two years ago, the first almost 20 years ago.
The cause that produced it was LSD.
His diagnosis is schizo-affective-paranoid psychosis, in addition he's maniac-depressive, he takes medication, against depression, mania, schizophrenia.
He's learned to live with his illness, he's been married for three years.
"In psychosis I've experienced the most terrible anguish and the deepest desperation," he says.
Hallucinations are a cruel reality.
Approximately one per cent of the population falls ill at least once in schizophrenia; psychiatric clinics are treated as the second most common disease after depression.
In acute schizophrenic psychosis, most of the time those affected can no longer distinguish between the outside world and the interior, and fall into a state of overexcitement that can lead to hallucinations, delusions of grandeur, relationship disorders, or persecutory mania.
Especially widespread is listening to voices: voices that give orders, comment or dialogue.
Optical hallucinations are also common.
The affected person is no longer able to distinguish the important from what isn't, the brain can no longer leak the disruptive signals, consciousness is flooded with impressions.
Like the dream, psychosis also opens the gates to a torrent of ideas and fantasies that come from the deepest layers of consciousness.
Percrestory of reality in psychosis is focused on the subject, says Professor Thomas Fuchs, chief medical officer at Heidelberg's psychiatry clinic.
Schizophrenic is at the centre of events, it all has a meaning addressed to it.
At the same time he enters into a passive role, such as a dream, feels overwhelmed by events and can't organize them actively.
That's why schizophrenic people often feel endangered, controlled and manipulated.
In general it is not possible to adjust the reality to them, hallucinations are a cruel reality, awakening, impossible.
You've experienced a hallucination once in your life.
Contrary rather than in a dream, says Fuchs, "the spatial and sensory structure of the reality that are perceived in psychosis remains in principle as it is."
Paranoid thoughts and hallucinations are based on reality, distancing the experience takes place in reality.
For the researchers there's still an unconscious why our brain makes goats.
According according to Fuchs, states of exception can lead anyone to lose contact with the reality on a temporary basis.
But not all people develop a psychosisis.
There appears to be a genetic layout for schizophrenia, but studies in twins have shown that in addition to genes there are also other determining factors.
Investigators assume that those affected suffer minimal brain damage.
"We suspect that neuronal maturity in the brain is affected at an early stage of development, such as through a mother's viral illness during pregnancy, Fuchs explains.
But it's still unacknowledg of how this alteration of the neuronal system occurs, which also hasn't manifested at all for many years.
The influence of the environment also plays an an important "stress or a traumatic experience can be the triggers of a psychosis when there is already a prior disposition.
Likewise, hallucinogenic drugs can cause psychosiphasis, interfering with the transmitting system of the brain.
When The Brain Cheats
The illogical thing about all this is that psychotic people suffer with their hallucinations and take very expensive medications to get rid of them, while others pay for drugs that allow them to experience some, says psychology professor Erich Kasten of Lebeck.
Perceiving hallucination as pleasant or doesn't often depend on the point of view.
Kasten has for ten years collected reports of hallucinations and visions and has analyzed the results in the book Die irreale Welt in unserem Kopf (The Unreal World in Our Head), published last year.
Hallucinations are among the typical accompanying symptoms of psychosis, but also, surprisingly, of many other diseases.
Among them are psychic disorders such as personality boundary disorder or depressions, diseases such as migraine, epilepsy, multiple sclerosis, Alzheimer' &apos; shoes and cranioencephalic trauma .
Surprisingly and under certain conditions, the brain of healthy people also suffers from hallucinations.
The hallucinations, according to Kasten, are composed of memorabilia that are normally repressed.
They arise when the areas of the brain in which sensory perceptions are processed are hyperactive or unprevented; when our brain warms, but also when stimuli are lacking.
The drugs can cause this state in the same way as brain damage or psychosisis.
But it's also stimuli restriction, the stress, the evil of love or other exceptional brain states.
In j?ngeren Studien gab bis zu einem Drittel der Befragten an, mindestens einmal im Leben eine Halluzination erlebt zu haben.
In the summer of 2005, Sarah K. was 16 years of age, lived in a small town of Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania and went to a secondary school.
An ordinary girl.
If it wasn't because of those pictures and the voices.
Sometimes, during recess, a clumsy kid with long-dauted dark hair addresses his word.
When she responds, the other students look at her surprised; no one, apart from her, hears or sees the kid.
They don't think the shy girl's a little weird, but Sarah K. She knows she's not dreaming. She's not dreaming.
She's awake and go and hear that kid, just as he sees and hear the girl sitting on the bench next door.
You may even feel your presence, at least a few moments.
It's a nice sensation, he likes the kid.
She sometimes wonders if she's crazy.
He also knows the kid and the rest of the visions aren't real and disappear.
At night, the apparitions are alarming.
Visments are often a burden.
When you're in class sometimes you cann't follow the lesson because the faculty to your eyes and e-ear is presenting a new partner, while for the rest it explains mathematical equities visibly and audible.
At night, the apparitions are alarming.
While he waits in the dark room for sleep to arrive, which often arrives late and is scarce, he does not know how to distinguish whether he is awakening or dreaming, if the images he seizes, grimaces or the girl with bleeding cuts on his arms and legs, appear in front of his eyes open or closed.
Leave me alone, he yells at the figures.
She hasn't been told by her parents and friends, you'd think she's crazy.
Autumn of 2009. Sarah K. He's 20 years old today.
Approved last year and lives with her boyfriend.
The hallucinations have disappeared for a year.
Either she's come into contact online with psychologist Kasten, she knows she's not crazy.
Craziness is also a piece of art out of desperation.
According to Kasten, when a person is socially isolated or suffers from a stimuli restriction, it is very likely that after a while he will start having hallucinations.
"I think the brain is unoccupied and you're responsible for looking for entertainment.
Sarah K., an only daughter, was scared of the other children and would often play lonely.
His hallucinations began when, at the age of 13, he learned that the man he calls Dad since he has used the reason is not his biological father.
Since visual and acoustic hallucinations are active in the same areas of the brain that also process real impressions and noisy, those affected such as Sarah K find it difficult to distinguish between visions and reality.Every experience," says Kasten, takes place in the brain, the reality is projected there.
The hallucinations are equally images that the brain produces, that's why they seem so real."
Kasten also classifies late-to-night fantasies as a form of hallucination.
The hallucinations of healthy people don't fundamentally differ from acute psychosis.
Unlike schizophrenic, psychicly healthy people are able to recognize their visions as unrealistic.
Kasten therefore advises its patients to apprehense their hallucinations as a special ability.
Limit situation between sleep and hallucination
In this world that is too conscious, imaginary experiences are usually pathologized, says Michael Schmidt-Degenhard, a professor in Heidelberg and chief physician of the psychiatry and psychotherapy clinic at Florence-Nightingale-Krankenhauses Hospital in Dusseldorf.
Psychiasis is a tough and painful disease but it also involves something positive and creative - craziness is an artwork of desperation.
Schmidt-Degenhard has investigated so-called oneirism, a special form of consciousness, which is perceived as a dream.
Swaminal hallucinations are almost very complex and detailed dreams that the person who experiences them considers to be real and who subsequently cannot differentiate from being awake.
Thirchy conscious states arise in extreme situations associated with a trauma or a disease, for example in patients with polyradiaphysiology, who experience motor paralysis being fully conscious. die bei vollem Bewusstsein eine motorische L.hmung erleben.
According to Schmidt-Degenhardt, when a person is threatened by a loss of oneself or of the world due to a total and permanent motor incapacity, it replaces a very dangerous real situation with its own and imaginary world that is decoupled from reality.
It's a form of boundary situation between sleep and hallucination.
Since it is not possible to overcome a situation in reality, there is a change of the world, although the real situation is still present in the imagination.
An intensive way of giving meaning and interpretation to an unbearable situation itself that our own capacity for creation allows us not to plunge into the nothingness of the loss of consciousness.
Everything is real, even later in memory
Art historian Peter Cornelius Claussen of Zurich also describes his dream experiences in his book Herzenwechsel as a soul-rescuing action.
He was 49 years old when his conscience embarked on several-ha-day trips following an operation of the heart-beathed heath-wheel-trengthened, while his body was doomed to immobility at the intensive care station.
Claussen immersed himself in a world created by himself and visited strange places of other times: he was stuck in bed, an older man aboard a sanatorium on a train, a robot watched for his health.
It was seen in the upper Middle Ages, surrounded by nobles that produced narcotics from blood.
A crook on a motorbike kidnapped him in his home country, surfed his hospital bed in the Alps with firn snow and met a Korean mobster.
Everything gives in these imaginary deeds, it's all real, even later in remembrance.
For Claussen, these indoor journeys are fundamentally different from any dream.
Dream is a different state, thus describing his or her experience.
On the contrary, the memories of mental travel are, many years later, much clearer, even in the irrelevant details.
It's even clearer and more intensive than real experiences.
"The intensity of this experience dilutes the horizon of the rest of our daily experiences," adds psychiatrist Schmidt-Degenhard.
Ultimately, oneirism, psychosis, hallucinations and the use of narcotics show just how fragiled the inner reproduction of external reality is, even when we are awake.
"Every modification of the state of consciousness," psychologist Kasten summarizes, "may lead to a deformation of what we call reality."
An experience that, on the other hand, can be utterly enriching.
