Burda
The children will take care of the publisher at the age of 27.
New publisher Kallen has come up with an interim solution: Hubert Burda wants to leave his company in the future in the hands of his two children, he told SPIEGEL.
100 per per cent of the holding company will also be in the hands of the family.
The publisher Hubert Burda sees his company in the hands of his children in the future.
The two sons become heirs at the age of 27 and have a firm willingness to join the company, Burda, 69, told SPIEGEL.
Details have been arranged as a precautious measure.
The next question is whether they will actively participate in the company.
Also, if you're trained to do so.
You can't put 7,500 workers in front of your children, if you're not trained enough.
That'd kill the kids and the company as well.
Burda announced on Thursday that he will rejoin the chair of the board from January and that Paul-Bernhard Kallen has been appointed his successor.
This member of the Kallen family, 52, has been a member of Burda's management since 1999.
He was initially responsible for tech.
Later the treasury was in charge of the treasury and thus came the responsibility for the company's assets. After the departure of J.rgen Todenhefer, he also took care of direct marketing, abroad, printing and finance.
For the first time, no members of the family occupy the peak of the publisher.
Burda's children are aged 19 and 17. His son Felix, who is currently 42, died a few years ago.
Kallen &apos; s appointment also serves to "short differences of 10, 15 years," according to Burda.
100 per per cent of the holding company will also be in the hands of the family.
Looking at the Schickedanz and Schaeffle cases, Burda stated that family businesses are also not safe from decay: "This has always affected families who let themselves be infected by the quest to search for capital, who have misjudged and thought that they could rise as the greatest player.
That has never been my strategy.
I've never had "delusions of grandeur," Burda says.
I don't see myself as a political preceptor of this country, as Springer was, and I also don't want to play with "Time" in the world league either; those businesses have become too big.
