The Promise of FonPeek
Peek is Extremely limited, a device that only serves to look at the mail, with no possibility to even see certain types of attachments or browse the network.
Enjoying his five-minor fame in the U.S. when Time magazine named him one of the products of the year.
To see him on the street is more difficult, the truth, but the idea can be interesting, especially for young people.
Unlike smartphones, Peek has a very low price and a very low monthly fee, less than half of what's paid, for example, by the iPhone's data plan.
In view of the success the company has released two new devices: one exclusively designed for Twitter, the other with a lifelong-for-ceilable data service in exchange for paying six times more for the Peek.
FonPeek, presented yesterday at the Le Web event, is the European version of the invention and its third incarnation.
It's just as limited as its American counterpart, but it's coming with a surprise.
The monthly maintenance price, of about 12 euros per month, includes roaming all over Europe.
Those who travel with a smartphone already know what it's like to give up the data when crossing the border or desperately searching for a cafeteria with Wi-Fi (or an Fon connection) to be able to check the mail.
The idea of fixed-price roaming is unbelievably attractive and would have to be in operation for a long time.
Yeah, some operators have special agreements and travel plans, but in my experience it takes a doctorate in physics to understand them and some good glasses to read the small print.
Repeated ear pulls by the European Commission to achieve affordable roaming rates across Europe have barely had a perceptible effect.
Out-of-the-hom country there's a scary about using the data, and rightly so.
The fact that an appliance like FonPeek (it's still curious to wear Fon's name when it doesn't lack Wifi) gets this type of tariff means that operators begin to think differently and study different proposals with which to amortize their data network.
Kindle is another good example, including book download traffic and other queries on the Internet.
No contracts and no bagpipes.
It's a connected device and it's paid for when it's bought, not month-on-month.
And that's what I'm hoping to see one day with FonPeek, a lifelong purchase option like Peek already has in the U.S.
Don't because I'm gonna buy it.
I'll look at my mail on the phone, thank you.
I'm waiting because I think it's a fairer model for the consumer.
It'd be exciting to see a computer with this same system.
