Seventy-five per 75 per cent of working mothers have had problems due to their motherhood
Discrimination at work has increased in recent years, especially for women of younger generations who want to be mothers.
This is revealed by the study 'Fashundity and work trajectory of women in Spain', prepared by the CSIC in collaboration with the Women's Institute.
The survey of 9,737 women between the ages of 15 and 75 leaves no hesitation: 75 per cent of working mothers have been limited in opportunities in the workplace due to motherhood.
The study points out that there are many differences in the beginning of cohabitation as a couple and the number of children, depending on whether or not they work and the type of work they carry out.
Those who don't have an out-of-the-home activity, work part-time, or have an unstable job, start cohabiting as a couple earlier, advance the first motherhood, and they have more children.
On the contrary, women in stable employment, mainly in the public sector, delay cohabitation and motherhood and have fewer offspring.
"Women between the ages of 35 and 49 with fixed work have the first child, on average, 3.7 years after starting cohabitation, a figure that increases to 4.1 women with fixed jobs have needed more time of their vital trajectory to achieve them, which has led them to postpone the union and the arrival of the children.
In addition, the type of work carried out exerts a lot of influence on motherhood although less on the schedule of pairing," explains CSIC researcher Margarita Delgado.
The level of studies is another factor that differentiates the age of women when they marry and having the first offspring.
According to the survey, the higher the level of education, the greater the age at which they are married and that of their first child.
The difference also remains between women of the same generation.
For example, in the group between 35 and 49 years of age, those with elementary and higher education have been mothers for the first time at 25,1 and 32.1 on average, respectively.
"Another division is also established among women according to their level of education.
While those who have elementary education marry and have their first child before getting a stable job, those of medium or higher levels invest this sequence and give priority to obtaining a stable job as a condition prior to marriage and the arrival of a child," Delgado says.
