How much do you should charge for your Facebook profile?
Users of Facebook will be able to know the salary they deserve according to their profile, thanks to a new Trovit application, the search engine with the largest number of classified real estate, employment and engine advertisements in Spain.
The Tool How Much Should You Receive? It calculates users' salaries from the more than 140, 000 job offers available to Trovit in Spain, based on a form available on Facebook, where they must introduce their profession, years of experience, age and city where they want to work.
In the week that the application has been in operation, the search engine has detected that generally the Spanish worker "feels poorly remunerated," Trovit's product manager, Albert Ribera, explained in a statement.
According to Trovit &apos; s accounts for its search engines in the United Kingdom and France, Spaniards are the lowest-paid.
A British computer programmer collects 45,226 gross euros a year and a French one, 31,059, compared to the 24,000 received by a Spaniard.
In the same way, a driver charges 34,247 euros net annually in England, 25,751 in France and 16,420 in Spain.
According to Trovit, the lowest remunerated work in Spain is waiter, with 11,592 net euros per year; followed by the dependent, with 14,725 euros; and that of operator, with 15,667 euros.
The most sought-after jobs in the network in the last three months are that of drivers, half-time jobs and that of social workers.
Brazil sets a date on Zelaya's departure
The Brazilian government has set a deadline for Manuel Zelaya's stay at its embassy in Tegucigalpa.
The deposed President must leave the diplomatic legation no later than 27 January 2010, when he would officially complete his term of office.
Francisco Catunda, in charge of Brazil's embassy business in the Honduran capital, confirmed to TV Globo that "Mel" knows he must leave the embassy before that date, where he has been a refugee since September 21.
"He is aware that he has to leave on January 27, at the end of his term, he has to look for another destiny."
The station also cited the former president, who confirmed his intention to leave the embassy before the established deadline: "My's position is to leave as soon as possible, logically with the support of the Brazilian government," Zelaya said by telephone.
After the November 29 elections (in which Porfirio Lobo was elected), Zelaya said he would remain at the Brazilian embassy until January.
This same week, however, he tried to seek refuge in Brazil.
The operation was finally thwarted in rejecting Zelaya's status as a political asylum seeker intended to be imposed on him by the "de facto" government presided over by Roberto Micheletti.
