Distributed
This apparent paradox is explained by UEFA's complicated money-sharing procedure.
For this financial year, Michel Platini's governing body plans to enter 1.090 million for the Champions League.
UEFA will dedicate 413.1 million for fixed payments: each of the 20 clubs that participated in the pre-qualification round (including Atlético de Madrid) received 2.1 million.
In addition, those who managed to participate received $3.8 million only for being there, in addition to an additional 550,000 euros for each party they dispute.
There's also bonus for winning (800,000 euros) and tying (400,000 for each team).The eighth finalists will receive three million each; the eight quarter-finalists, 3.3 million.
The last four will pocket $4 million each.
The champion will earn 9 million, compared to 5.2 million of the finalist.
Thus, in terms of fixed payments, a club will receive at least 7.1 million, and a maximum of 31,2 million.
On the other hand, $2.5 million was bagged by winning the European Super Cup in August.
But there are also the variable payments, to which $337.8 million is allocated.
This money is distributed according to the proportional value of each television market.
Spaniard is one of the main, and has four assigned places.
The champion of the previous year stays with 40 per cent of the money allocated, 30 per per cent for the second, 20 per cent for the third and 10 per cent for the fourth.
The other part will be paid according to the number of matches played in this year's edition.
